[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001358","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001358","source_record_id":"rec_001358","source_corpus":"include","title":"Harnessing the Power of Single-Cell Large Language Models with Parameter Efficient Fine-Tuning using scPEFT","abstract":"","doi":"10.1038/s42256-025-01170-z","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"selected_full_text_sections":"[data_source, input_representation: Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT > Supplementary Notes > Supplementary Note 8: Unsupervised adaptation of scPEFT to the lung cancer atlas]\nTo further validate the generalizability of our unsupervised adaptation protocol, we conducted an additional experiment on a comprehensive lung cancer atlas 13 that includes over 1.2 million cells from 19 studies and 309 patients. This atlas provides hierarchical cell ontology annotations and rich metadata describing diverse cellular populations at varying levels of granularity. To ensure an out-of-context scenario for the scGPT backbone in the unsupervised protocol, we excluded cells from healthy controls.\n\nIn  this  experiment, only the gene expression profiles were used as input to the unsupervised adaptation protocol. The resulting cell embeddings generated by native scGPT, finetuned scGPT, and scPEFT were visualized using UMAP, with cell colors reflecting the hierarchical annotations. This  allowed  us  to  assess  how  well  each  model  captured  cellular  structure  and  revealed subpopulations within the disease atlas.\n\nFrom the Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection UMAP visualizations ( Supplementary Fig. 21a ), we observed clear clustering of major cell types, even in the zero-shot embeddings from native scGPT, highlighting the strong biological prior captured during pretraining. Following adaptation, both finetuned scGPT and scPEFT further refined the separation of major cell types and  introduced  additional  subclusters.  Higher  Calinski-Harabasz  index  (CHI)  scores  for  the adapted  models  confirmed  improved  cluster  separability  and  cohesiveness.  These  emergent subclusters suggest that context-specific biological signals were captured during unsupervised adaptation.\n\nTo further investigate these subpopulations, we applied finer annotations to color the major cell types ( Supplementary Fig. 21b ). Within the epithelial compartment, which includes tumor cells, scPEFT  and  finetuned  scGPT  revealed  clear  boundaries  between  alveolar,  transitional,  and cancer cells, separations not apparent in native scGPT embeddings, indicating that the adapted models generated more context-aware representations. Moreover, the cancer cell population split into numerous distinct subclusters, each corresponding to its patient of origin and associated with varying Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) stages ( Supplementary Fig. 21c ). This pattern  is  expected  because  the  profile  of  genomic  alterations  in  each  tumor  will  be  unique, making a spread of augmentations to the baseline gene expression state.\n\nThis trend was consistently observed across other major cell types with finer cell type annotations in  the  atlas  ( Supplementary  Figs.  22-23 ).  Across  these  cases,  scPEFT  generally  achieved higher CHI scores than finetuned scGPT, particularly for dominant cell groups, including CD4 + T cells,  alveolar  macrophages,  and  lymphatic  endothelial  cells.  These  results  suggest  that compared to the finetuning strategy, scPEFT better preserves the overall structure of cell types while  offering  discerning  sensitivity  to  biologically  meaningful  subpopulations  in  large-scale disease contexts.","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001358","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary model paper on scPEFT for single-cell data, and the full text says only gene expression profiles were used as input. No substantive natural-language/text component or text-bio bridge is evidenced.","evidence_snippet":"\"only the gene expression profiles were used as input\"; \"native scGPT\"; \"cell embeddings\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001358","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The record centers on parameter-efficient fine-tuning of an existing scGPT backbone, so it reads as an application/wrapper rather than a new primary model paper. It also describes broad pretraining and transfer across a large lung cancer atlas, but does not clearly state a generative mechanism in the provided sections.","evidence_snippet":"\"single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT\"; \"native scGPT backbone\"; \"strong biological prior captured during pretraining\"; \"generalizability\"; \"cell embeddings generated by native scGPT\"; \"large-scale disease contexts\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]

