./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0065.stderr.log:64:[{"record_id":"rec_001345","title":"Abstract 1143: Dissection of melanoma and cutaneous lymphoma spatial molecular architecture by multimodal single-cell and spatial transcriptomics with generative AI","abstract":"Advances in single-cell multiomic technologies have revolutionized our understanding of how heterogeneous cell types and cell states shape the tumor microenvironment (TME). However, dissociated single cell profiling lacks spatial context. To resolve the organization, cell-cell communication, and granular structures in the TME, new single-cell spatial transcriptomic (ST) characterizations are needed. Here, we dissected the spatial molecular architecture of the melanoma (30 tumors, 30 patients) and transformed cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (tCTCL, 12 tumors, 6 patients) TMEs by Multiplexed Error-Robust FISH (305 gene MERFISH), with benchmarking scRNAseq data from matched tissues (6 melanoma scFFPEseq, 6 tCTCL scVDJ/RNAseq) for cross-platform validation. We profiled 565,122 cells in melanoma and 92584 cells in tCTCL by MERFISH. We interrogated both datasets using a novel computational framework that includes cell typing by Leiden clustering and marker genes, spatial neighborhood and receptor-ligand (R-L) analyses, and spatial/scRNA imputation via deep learning. In the melanoma dataset, we identified tumor cells, major TME cell types, and rare immune cell types (TCF7+ stem-like T-cells and CD3+TCRα+PAX5+CD79a+ B/T dual-expressor lymphocytes enriched in tertiary lymphoid structures). All cell types were validated in scFFPEseq. As immune R-L interactions are critical therapeutic targets, we developed a novel computation method to quantify spatial R-L interactions by accounting for cell-cell distance, identifying spatially informed R-L pairs that differ from dissociation-based inferences. CNV inference from melanoma scFFPEseq revealed intratumoral heterogeneity (ITH). We therefore adapted the conditional variational autoencoder ENVI to simultaneously incorporate scRNA and MERFISH spatial data into a unified latent embedding. ENVI-ITH successfully learned ITH subgroup information, enabling imputation of phylogenetic lineages on spatial MERFISH. CTCL MERFISH data revealed major TME cell types, yet separation of malignant vs benign T-cells by clustering and marker approaches is hindered by their overlapping gene expression. To overcome this barrier, we deployed ENVI to impute patient-specific malignant and benign TCR clonotypes by training on the matched scVDJ/RNA data, yielding an ENVI-TCR pipeline that can delineate malignant vs benign T-cells in situ. We validated the spatial pattern of malignant T-cells by patient-specific in situ TCR probes. In sum, we have interrogated the melanoma and CTCL TME by MERFISH and presented a novel computational framework for robustly co-embedding scRNA data with imaging-based ST to spatially resolve melanoma ITH and malignant/benign T-cell populations. We believe this approach will be highly impactful for melanoma and CTCL and can be broadly applied to other solid tumors and lymphomas.\n Citation Format: Zichao Liu, Xiaofei Song, Jiang He, Justin He, Jodi Balasi, Jeffrey H. Chuang, Pei-Ling Chen. Dissection of melanoma and cutaneous lymphoma spatial molecular architecture by multimodal single-cell and spatial transcriptomics with generative AI [abstract]. In: Proceedings of the American Association for Cancer Research Annual Meeting 2024; Part 1 (Regular Abstracts); 2024 Apr 5-10; San Diego, CA. Philadelphia (PA): AACR; Cancer Res 2024;84(6_Suppl):Abstract nr 1143.","doi":"10.1158/1538-7445.am2024-1143","year":"2024","venue":"Cancer Research","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001345","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Primary computational framework over MERFISH/scRNA-seq spatial transcriptomics. No substantive text or language component is described.","evidence_snippet":"“multimodal single-cell and spatial transcriptomics with generative AI” and “deep learning” for scRNA and MERFISH"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001345","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly describes ENVI as a conditional variational autoencoder, which is generative. It claims broad applicability, but does not clearly show broad pretraining or foundation-model-like transfer evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"adapted the conditional variational autoencoder ENVI\" and \"can be broadly applied to other solid tumors and lymphomas\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001348","title":"Foundation models in pathology: bridging AI innovation and clinical practice","abstract":"Foundation models are revolutionising pathology by leveraging large-scale, pretrained artificial intelligence (AI) systems to enhance diagnostics, automate workflows and expand applications. These models address computational challenges in gigapixel whole-slide images with architectures like GigaPath, enabling state-of-the-art performance in cancer subtyping and biomarker identification by capturing cellular variations and microenvironmental changes. Visual-language models such as CONCH integrate histopathological images with biomedical text, facilitating text-to-image retrieval and classification with minimal fine-tuning, mirroring how pathologists synthesise multimodal information. Open-source foundation models will drive accessibility and innovation, allowing researchers to refine AI systems collaboratively while reducing dependency on proprietary solutions. Combined with decentralised learning approaches like federated and swarm learning, these models enable secure, large-scale training without centralised data sharing, preserving patient confidentiality while improving generalisability across populations. Despite these advancements, challenges remain in ensuring scalability, mitigating bias and aligning AI insights with clinical decision-making. Explainable AI techniques, such as saliency maps and feature attribution, are critical for fostering trust and interpretability. As multimodal integration—combining pathology, radiology and genomics—advances personalised medicine, foundation models stand as a transformative force in computational pathology, bridging the gap between AI innovation and real-world clinical implementation.","doi":"10.1136/jcp-2024-209910","year":"2025","venue":"Journal of Clinical Pathology","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001348","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract is a review/editorial on pathology foundation models rather than a primary model report. It explicitly discusses visual-language models integrating pathology images and biomedical text.","evidence_snippet":"“Foundation models are revolutionising pathology” and “visual-language models ... integrate histopathological images with biomedical text”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001348","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a review of foundation models in pathology rather than an original model paper. It discusses pretrained systems generally, but does not describe a specific candidate model with extractable generative or FM evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"Foundation models are revolutionising pathology\" and \"bridging AI innovation and clinical practice\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001356","title":"SeqMate: A Novel Large Language Model Pipeline for Automating RNA Sequencing","abstract":"RNA sequencing techniques, like bulk RNA-seq and Single Cell (sc) RNA-seq, are critical tools for the biologist looking to analyze the genetic activity/transcriptome of a tissue or cell during an experimental procedure. Platforms like Illumina's next-generation sequencing (NGS) are used to produce the raw data for this experimental procedure. This raw FASTQ data must then be prepared via a complex series of data manipulations by bioinformaticians. This process currently takes place on an unwieldy textual user interface like a terminal/command line that requires the user to install and import multiple program packages, preventing the untrained biologist from initiating data analysis. Open-source platforms like Galaxy have produced a more user-friendly pipeline, yet the visual interface remains cluttered and highly technical, remaining uninviting for the natural scientist. To address this, SeqMate is a user-friendly tool that allows for one-click analytics by utilizing the power of a large language model (LLM) to automate both data preparation and analysis (differential expression, trajectory analysis, etc). Furthermore, by utilizing the power of generative AI, SeqMate is also capable of analyzing such findings and producing written reports of upregulated/downregulated/user-prompted genes with sources cited from known repositories like PubMed, PDB, and Uniprot.","doi":"10.20944/preprints202407.0160.v1","year":"2024","venue":"","sources":["arxiv","biorxiv_medrxiv","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001356","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"SeqMate wraps an existing LLM to automate RNA-seq workflows and generate written reports. The LLM is used as an interface/reporting layer rather than a substantive text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"utilizing the power of a large language model (LLM) to automate both data preparation and analysis\"; \"producing written reports\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001356","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"SeqMate is a pipeline that uses a large language model to automate RNA-seq analysis and generate written reports. The abstract does not give enough detail about the underlying model’s pretraining or foundation-model characteristics.","evidence_snippet":"“utilizing the power of a large language model (LLM)” and “producing written reports”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001357","title":"Whole Genome Sequencing of Enterococcus faecalis Isolated from Stool Sample of a Postmenopausal Women with Breast Cancer Patient in Malaysia","abstract":"Breast cancer stands as a formidable and prevalent cause of mortality among women in Malaysia, prompting rigorous research into potential contributors to its development. Given the well-established association between gut microbiota and various forms of cancer, a particular focus has been placed on exploring the role of Enterococcus in breast cancer pathogenesis. Several studies have identified Enterococcus as a significant component of the estrobolome, the collection of gut microbiota involved in the metabolism of estrogens. This association has garnered attention due to its potential link to breast cancer. The estrobolome's role in modulating estrogen levels in the body suggests that Enterococcus could influence breast cancer risk by affecting estrogen homeostasis. Thus, this study endeavours to unravel the potential implications of Enterococcus faecalis in breast cancer by delving into its genome and decoding gene functions through bioinformatics analysis. Whole genome sequencing emerged as the methodological linchpin, revealing a distinctive gene expression profile within Enterococcus faecalis. The size of draft genome sequenced was found to be 2.8Mb, with a GC content of 37.7%, and 4430 protein coding sequences were detected within the genome. Notably, the bacterium sequence subjected to gene annotation exhibited an expression of β-glucosidase, an enzyme intricately involved in the deconjugation of estrogen. While these findings underscore the plausible contribution of Enterococcus faecalis to breast cancer, a considerable knowledge gap persists regarding the genetic variations within specific strains of this bacterium. As such, a more nuanced and comprehensive exploration is warranted to bridge this existing gap in our understanding.","doi":"10.15282/cst.v4i2.11856","year":"2024","venue":"Current Science and Technology","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001357","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a whole-genome sequencing and annotation study, not a candidate generative model paper. No substantive text or language component is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"Whole Genome Sequencing of Enterococcus faecalis\"; \"gene annotation\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001357","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract is about whole-genome sequencing and bioinformatics annotation of a bacterial isolate. It does not describe any generative model or foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“Whole genome sequencing emerged as the methodological linchpin” and “bioinformatics analysis”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001358","title":"Harnessing the Power of Single-Cell Large Language Models with Parameter Efficient Fine-Tuning using scPEFT","abstract":"He et al. present a parameter-efficient fine-tuning method for single-cell language models that improves performance on unseen diseases, treatments and cell types. Single-cell large language models (scLLMs) capture essential biological insights from vast single-cell atlases but struggle in out-of-context applications, where zero-shot predictions can be unreliable. To address this, here we introduce a single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework that integrates learnable, low-dimensional adapters into scLLMs. By freezing the backbone model and updating only the adapter parameters, scPEFT efficiently adapts to specific tasks using limited custom data. This approach mitigates catastrophic forgetting, reduces parameter tuning by over 96% and decreases GPU memory usage by more than half, thus substantially enhancing the accessibility of scLLMs for resource-constrained researchers. When validated across diverse datasets, scPEFT outperformed zero-shot models and traditional fine-tuning in disease-specific, cross-species and undercharacterized cell population tasks. Its attention-mechanism analysis identified COVID-related genes associated with specific cell states and uncovered unique blood cell subpopulations, demonstrating the capacity of scPEFT for condition-specific interpretations. These findings position scPEFT as an efficient solution for enhancing the utility of scLLMs in general single-cell analyses.","doi":"10.1038/s42256-025-01170-z","year":"2025","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["google_scholar","semantic_scholar","springernature"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001358","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This presents a parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework for single-cell large language models. The abstract clearly links the text/modeling component to biological single-cell data and downstream cell-state tasks.","evidence_snippet":"\"single-cell large language models (scLLMs)\"; \"parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001358","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework built on existing single-cell large language models. The abstract explicitly references broad zero-shot use and adapting a frozen backbone rather than introducing a new base model.","evidence_snippet":"“single-cell large language models (scLLMs)” and “freezing the backbone model and updating only the adapter parameters”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001359","title":"NicheTrans: Spatial-aware Cross-omics Translation","abstract":"ABSTRACT Spatial omics technologies have revolutionized our studies on tissue architecture and cellular interactions at single-cell resolution. While spatial multi-omics approaches offer unprecedented insights into complex biological systems, their widespread adoption is hindered by technical challenges, specialized requirements, and limited accessibility. To address these limitations, we present NicheTrans, the first spatially-aware cross-omics translation method and a flexible Transformer-based multi-modal deep learning framework. Unlike existing single-cell (non-spatial) translation methods, NicheTrans uniquely incorporates both cellular microenvironment information and flexible integration of multi-modal data, such as morphology and prior knowledge. We validated NicheTrans across diverse biological cases: Parkinson’s Disease (PD), Alzheimer’s Disease (AD), breast cancer, and lymph nodes. Our approach demonstrated superior performance compared to existing single-cell methods, highlighting the crucial role of spatial and multi-modal information in cross-omics translation. Through NicheTrans, we uncovered spatial multi-omics domains that were not detectable through single-omics analysis alone. Model interpretation revealed key molecular relationships, including gene programs associated with dopamine metabolism and amyloid β-associated cell states. Additionally, using translated protein markers as spatial landmarks, we quantified the spatial organization of key glial cell subtypes in the AD brain. NicheTrans represents a powerful tool for generating comprehensive spatial multi-omics insights from more accessible single-omics measurements, making multi-omics analysis more feasible for the broader research community.","doi":"10.1101/2024.12.05.626986","year":"2024","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["biorxiv_medrxiv","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001359","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"NicheTrans is a transformer-based multi-modal framework for spatial omics translation. The abstract does not describe a substantive text or language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"spatially-aware cross-omics translation method\"; \"Transformer-based multi-modal deep learning framework\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001359","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"NicheTrans is presented as a Transformer-based cross-omics translation framework, but the abstract does not explicitly state a decoder, autoregressive, or seq2seq generative mechanism. It also does not provide evidence of broad pretraining or foundation-model-like scope.","evidence_snippet":"“first, spatially-aware cross-omics translation method” and “Transformer-based multi-modal deep learning framework”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0065.stderr.log:1743:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0208.response.txt:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_001657","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Abstract frames the work as benchmarking BCR sequencing technologies and explicitly says it generates a resource for the community. It is about immune repertoire sequencing, not a text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“benchmarking experiment” and “generated a resource for the immunology community”"},{"record_id":"rec_001658","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a Comment that discusses the landscape and barriers around single-cell large language models rather than presenting a new model. It mentions biomedical single-cell omics and LLMs, but not a specific primary text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"“In this Comment, we examine the current landscape of scLLMs”"},{"record_id":"rec_001659","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_component_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract reads as a forward-looking commentary on virtual cells, organs, and foundation models rather than a primary model paper. It does not clearly specify a substantive text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"“Navigating the Deep Waters of Medical AI Models” and “Ultimately, the trajectory...”"},{"record_id":"rec_001660","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This presents a new generative self-expression model for single-cell and spatial multimodal data. The abstract describes omics integration and representation learning, but no substantive text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"“we propose a contrastive and generative deep self-expression model” for “single-cell and spatial multimodal data”"},{"record_id":"rec_001661","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The work reuses pre-trained DNA language models and compares embedding-based pipelines versus fine-tuning for downstream genomic prediction. That is an application of existing models, not a new substantive text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“Large pre-trained DNA language models” and “embedding-based pipelines”"},{"record_id":"rec_001662","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"FEDKEA integrates a pretrained protein language model with KNN for enzyme annotation, which is a downstream application pipeline around existing model embeddings. The abstract does not support a new text-bio foundation model with a substantive bridge.","evidence_snippet":"“integrates ESM-2 and distance-weighted KNN” for “enzyme function annotation”"},{"record_id":"rec_001663","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a genomic data compression/encoding paper, not a generative text-bio model paper. The abstract describes direct coding of DNA bases and does not mention any text or language component.","evidence_snippet":"“two-bit coding” for “genomic sequence” compression"},{"record_id":"rec_001664","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says this review synthesizes the technology and applications of droplet-based scRNA-seq. It is a review, not a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"“This review synthesizes the technological evolution” of droplet-based single-cell RNA sequencing"}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0065.stderr.log:3577:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_001345","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes MERFISH/scRNA analysis with a conditional variational autoencoder, but no substantive text or language component is described. This is a biological generative model paper, not a text-bio foundation model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"multimodal single-cell and spatial transcriptomics with generative AI\"; \"adapted the conditional variational autoencoder ENVI\""},{"record_id":"rec_001348","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly frames the article as a review of foundation models in pathology rather than a primary model report. It discusses multimodal pathology-text models in review context, but does not describe a specific candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"Foundation models are revolutionising pathology\"; \"visual-language models such as CONCH integrate histopathological images with biomedical text\""},{"record_id":"rec_001356","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"SeqMate uses an existing LLM to automate RNA-seq preparation, analysis, and reporting. The abstract presents a workflow wrapper around the model rather than a substantive text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"utilizing the power of a large language model (LLM) to automate both data preparation and analysis\"; \"producing written reports\""},{"record_id":"rec_001357","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a whole-genome sequencing and gene-annotation study of a bacterial isolate. No text or language component is described, and it is not clearly a model paper type.","evidence_snippet":"\"Whole Genome Sequencing of Enterococcus faecalis\"; \"gene annotation\""},{"record_id":"rec_001358","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract presents a new parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework for single-cell large language models. It clearly links a language-model backbone to biological single-cell tasks and downstream transfer settings.","evidence_snippet":"\"single-cell large language models (scLLMs)\"; \"freezing the backbone model and updating only the adapter parameters\""},{"record_id":"rec_001359","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"NicheTrans is a biological cross-omics translation framework, but the abstract does not describe any text or language component. The model is transformer-based, but generative behavior is not explicitly stated.","evidence_snippet":"\"spatially-aware cross-omics translation method\"; \"Transformer-based multi-modal deep learning framework\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0047.stderr.log:1306:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0012.prompt.txt:52:[{"record_id":"rec_000240","title":"Agentic Authoring of Interactive Multiview Visualizations in Genomics","abstract":"Diverse genomics data, scientific questions, and analysis tasks typically demand highly specialized visualizations. Therefore, users often must customize or author new ones tailored to their data. Existing tools are usually either limited in customization or require substantial learning or programming, and even expressive tools assume visualization expertise many users lack. Agentic and large language model (LLM) approaches are increasingly applied to complex scientific tasks, including visualization. Natural-language conversational interfaces offer a promising path to democratizing the authoring of complex visualizations. In the context of genomics, these approaches face additional challenges: genomics visualizations typically integrate heterogeneous data types and are composed of multiple linked interactive views. These challenges motivate more structured LLM-based schemes. We first characterize where vanilla LLM generation succeeds and fails for genomics visualization, identifying eight quality dimensions. We then compare six schemes--direct generation, a fixed pipeline, and four agentic configurations varying in the number of specialist agents and the presence of a reviewer--across 159 cases spanning three levels of query ambiguity and specification complexity. All schemes use the Gosling visualization grammar as structured output. Agentic iteration substantially improves perceived quality over both baselines, while more complex agent architectures yield no additional benefit. We discuss implications for designing agentic systems for domain-specific visualization authoring. All supplemental materials are available at https://osf.io/uqe83.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["arxiv"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000240","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The work studies agentic LLM schemes for authoring genomics visualizations, not a candidate bio-foundational model. The LLM is used as a tooling layer for visualization generation.","evidence_snippet":"Agentic and large language model (LLM) approaches are increasingly applied to complex scientific tasks, including visualization"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000240","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper studies LLM-based generation of visualization grammars, so a generative workflow is present. But it is an agentic authoring/application paper, and the abstract does not establish foundation-model-style pretraining for a candidate text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“vanilla LLM generation” and “All schemes use the Gosling visualization grammar as structured output”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000249","title":"Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT","abstract":"Abstract\n                Single-cell large language models (scLLMs) capture essential biological insights from vast single-cell atlases but struggle in out-of-context applications, where zero-shot predictions can be unreliable. To address this, we introduce a single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework that integrates learnable, low-dimensional adapters into scLLMs. By freezing the backbone model and updating only the adapter parameters, scPEFT efficiently adapts to specific tasks using limited custom data. This approach mitigates catastrophic forgetting, reduces parameter tuning by over 96%, and decreases GPU memory usage by more than half, significantly enhancing scLLMs’s accessibility for resource-constrained researchers. Validated across diverse datasets, scPEFT outperformed zero-shot models and traditional fine-tuning in disease-specific, cross-species, and under-characterized cell population tasks. Its attention-mechanism analysis identified COVID-related genes associated with specific cell states and uncovered unique blood cell subpopulations, demonstrating scPEFT’s capacity for condition-specific interpretations. These findings position scPEFT as an efficient solution for improving scLLMs’ utilities in general single-cell analyses.","doi":"10.1101/2025.04.21.649754","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Machine …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000249","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Introduces scPEFT as a new framework for single-cell large language models with learnable adapters. The model is explicitly for single-cell biological data and fine-tunes scLLMs for downstream tasks.","evidence_snippet":"\"single-cell large language models (scLLMs)\" and \"single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000249","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes parameter-efficient fine-tuning of existing scLLMs rather than a new generative model. It mentions LLMs, but the candidate here is a tuning framework, not a foundation model itself.","evidence_snippet":"“parameter-efficient fine-tuning ... integrates learnable, low-dimensional adapters into scLLMs”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000250","title":"scpFormer: A Foundation Model for Unified Representation and Integration of the Single-Cell Proteomics","abstract":"The integration of single-cell proteomic data is often hindered by the fragmented nature of   , a transformer-based foundation model designed for single-cell proteomics. Pre-trained on","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"arXiv preprint arXiv …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000250","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Presents a transformer-based foundation model for single-cell proteomics. The abstract does not describe any substantive text or language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"a transformer-based foundation model designed for single-cell proteomics\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000250","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","dec