act mentions it is pre-trained on a massive collection of gene sets. The provided text does not clearly describe a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"“gene set foundation model pre-trained on a massive collection of diverse gene sets”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000254","title":"ChromFound: Towards A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data","abstract":"spatial and temporal gene expression patterns [31]. Identifying OCRs at singlecell level is   -scale pretrained deep language model for cell type annotation of single-cell rna-seq data.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Advances in …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000254","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"ChromFound is presented as a universal foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data. The record supports a biological modality but not a text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000254","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title presents ChromFound as a universal foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data. The abstract snippet does not give enough mechanism detail to confirm a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"“Towards A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000256","title":"AICellType: a large language model-based platform for accurate cell type annotation","abstract":"Abstract\n                  Accurate cell type annotation is critical for studying cellular heterogeneity in single-cell and spatial transcriptomics. However, existing methods largely rely on static gene markers, limiting adaptability to diverse biological contexts and data types. To overcome this limitation, we systematically benchmarked 79 large language models (LLMs) over 1130 single-cell and spatial transcriptomics datasets using an evaluation framework combining ontology structure and semantic reasoning to quantify model performance in biological relevance and annotation robustness. Claude 3.5 Sonnet achieved the best overall performance, balancing weighted accuracy (76%), robustness, inference speed, and cost-efficiency. Based on these findings, we developed AICellType (https://AICellType.jinlab.online), a free, open-source R package and web platform that integrates seamlessly with Seurat workflows, supports multiple species and tissues, and enables flexible model deployment via OpenRouter or custom APIs. By leveraging LLMs’ capacity to interpret marker–cell type associations, AICellType provides a scalable, efficient, and accessible solution for real-world cell annotation in both single-cell and spatial omics research.","doi":"10.1093/bib/bbag151","year":"2026","venue":"Briefings in …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000256","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract centers on benchmarking 79 large language models across 1130 transcriptomics datasets and releasing a platform. This is primarily a benchmark/resource and application platform paper, not a new candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically benchmarked 79 large language models\" and \"developed AICellType ... a free, open-source R package and web platform\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000256","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a benchmarking study over 79 LLMs and a deployed platform for cell type annotation. It does not present a new generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“we systematically benchmarked 79 large language models” and “developed AICellType ... a ... platform”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0211.stderr.log:66:[{"record_id":"rec_003850","title":"Evaluating the learnability of single-cell large language models on multiple tasks","abstract":"Abstract\n                The rise of single-cell foundation models (scFMs) has sparked interest in their potential to unify diverse biological tasks. However, their practical utility and the validity of scaling laws—the assumption that performance improves with model and data size—remain under-examined. Here, we systematically evaluate two representative scFMs, Geneformer and scGPT, across perturbation prediction and cell type annotation tasks. Our findings suggest that the benefits of large-scale pretraining are strongly task-dependent, conferring substantial advantages in cell type annotation but limited gains in perturbation prediction. Furthermore, our results indicate that increasing model size does not guarantee improved performance and can even be detrimental, challenging the ``bigger is better'' paradigm. By comparing model performance on real versus synthetic data with different levels of complexity, our analysis suggests that for perturbation prediction, the tested scFMs capture little more than simple summary statistics and may struggle to learn complex biological interactions. These results highlight the need to move beyond scaling and toward developing models that integrate deeper biological knowledge. We suggest that a renewed focus on task-specific architectures and biologically-informed priors may be critical for unlocking the true potential of foundation models in single-cell biology.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003850","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an evaluation study of existing single-cell large language models across tasks, rather than a new model paper. It benchmarks Geneformer and scGPT on perturbation prediction and cell type annotation.","evidence_snippet":"“systematically evaluate two representative scFMs, Geneformer and scGPT” across tasks"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003850","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper evaluates existing single-cell large language models rather than introducing a new model. It clearly discusses large-scale pretraining and task transfer, but the abstract does not explicitly establish a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically evaluate two representative scFMs, Geneformer and scGPT\" and \"benefits of large-scale pretraining\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003853","title":"From Modality Translation to Regulatory Reasoning: Interpretable AI for Single-Cell Multi-Omic Systems","abstract":"Building on this foundation, we contribute to population- 42 ix 3.4 Determining cell type–specific  eQTLs from single-cell  commonly utilized in natural language processing models as well","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003853","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_ra