"rec_000252","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Introduces xVERSE as a transcriptomics-native foundation model for representation learning and virtual cell synthesis. The abstract explicitly contrasts it with language-model architectures, so no text component is supported here.","evidence_snippet":"\"a transcriptomics-native foundation model\" and \"Current single-cell foundation models rely on language-model architectures\""},{"record_id":"rec_000253","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title indicates a gene set foundation model, but the record does not support a substantive text or language component. It is centered on biological gene sets rather than text-bio bridging.","evidence_snippet":"\"gene set foundation model pre-trained on a massive collection of diverse gene sets\""},{"record_id":"rec_000254","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"ChromFound is presented as a universal foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data. The record supports a biological modality but not a text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data\""},{"record_id":"rec_000255","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"FLAG is a foundation model for spatial gene expression prediction, which is a biological modeling task. The abstract does not support any substantive text component.","evidence_snippet":"\"Foundation model representation ... for spatial gene expression prediction\""},{"record_id":"rec_000256","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract centers on benchmarking 79 large language models across 1130 transcriptomics datasets and releasing a platform. This is primarily a benchmark/resource and application platform paper, not a new candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically benchmarked 79 large language models\" and \"developed AICellType ... a free, open-source R package and web platform\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0082.stderr.log:65:[{"record_id":"rec_001656","title":"Machine learning enhances biomarker discovery: From multi- omics to functional genomics.","abstract":"Importance: Biomarkers are critical for precision medicine, supporting disease diagnosis, prognosis, personalized treatments, and monitoring. Traditional biomarker discovery methods, which often focus on single genes or proteins, face several challenges, including limited reproducibility, a limited ability to integrate multiple data streams, high false-positive rates, and inadequate predictive accuracy. Machine learning and deep learning methods, and large language models, paired with advancements in omics technologies, address these limitations by analyzing large, complex multi-omics datasets to identify more reliable and clinically useful biomarkers. Observations: Machine learning and deep learning have proven effective in biomarker discovery by integrating diverse and high-volume data types, such as genomics, transcriptomics, proteomics, metabolomics, imaging, and clinical records. These approaches successfully identify diagnostic, prognostic, and predictive biomarkers across fields, such as oncology, infectious diseases, neurological disorders, and autoimmune diseases. Newer methodological developments include approaches to identify functional biomarkers, notably biosynthetic gene clusters, crucial for discovering antibiotics and anticancer drugs. Key artificial intelligence (AI) techniques include neural networks, transformers, large language models, and feature selection methods, which are finding more and more application to omics data and in clinical settings. However, challenges remain regarding data quality, biological complexity, model interpretability, validation, and generalization. Regulatory and ethical considerations also impact clinical adoption, emphasizing the importance of validated, trustworthy, and explainable AI methods. Conclusions and Relevance: Machine learning, deep learning, and AI agent-based approaches significantly enhance biomarker discovery, providing valuable biological insights and advancing precision medicine. Future research should focus on directly linking genomic data to functional outcomes, particularly with biosynthetic gene clusters and non- coding RNAs. Rigorous validation, model interpretability, and regulatory compliance are essential for clinical implementation. These advancements promise to improve personalized treatment strategies and patient outcomes.","doi":"10.18103/mra.v13i8.6888","year":"2025","venue":"Medical Research Archives","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001656","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a review-style piece on biomarker discovery, summarizing machine learning, deep learning, and LLM applications. The abstract does not describe a single primary text-bio model with its own bridge architecture.","evidence_snippet":"\"Machine learning enhances biomarker discovery: From multi- omics to functional genomics.\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001656","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"This is a review-style article on biomarker discovery rather than a single model study. It discusses transformers and large language models broadly, but does not present a specific generative foundation model for screening.","evidence_snippet":"\"Machine learning, deep learning, and AI agent-based approaches\" and \"Key artificial intelligence techniques include neural networks, transformers, large language models\"."},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001658","title":"Overcoming barriers to the wide adoption of single-cell large language models in biomedical research","abstract":"Transformer-based large language models are gaining traction in biomedical research, particularly in single-cell omics. Despite their promise, the application of single-cell large language models (scLLMs) remains limited in practice. In this Comment, we examine the current landscape of scLLMs and the benchmark studies that assess their applications in various analytical tasks. We highlight existing technical gaps and practical barriers, and discuss future directions toward a more accessible and effective ecosystem to promote the applications of scLLMs in the biomedical community.","doi":"10.1038/s41587-025-02846-y","year":"2025","venue":"Nature Biotechnology","sources":["google_scholar","semantic_scholar","springernature"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001658","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a Comment that discusses the landscape and barriers around single-cell large language models rather than presenting a new model. It mentions biomedical single-cell omics and LLMs, but not a specific primary text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"“In this Comment, we examine the current landscape of scLLMs”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001658","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"This is a Comment on the landscape of single-cell large language models rather than a primary model report. It mentions scLLMs and benchmark studies, but does not describe a specific candidate model or its training.","evidence_snippet":"\"In this Comment, we examine the current landscape of scLLMs\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001659","title":"From Virtual Cell Challenge to Virtual Organs: Navigating the Deep Waters of Medical AI Models","abstract":"Recent advances in single-cell profiling and artificial intelligence have enabled the construction of virtual cells, computational entities that simulate cellular states and responses with unprecedented resolution. Initiatives such as the Virtual Cell World Challenge highlight the potential of large AI models to move beyond annotation toward predictive and generative simulations. Yet, the next frontier lies in scaling from virtual cells to virtual organs, where thousands of cell types interact across spatial, temporal, and biophysical dimensions. This transition exposes major challenges: incomplete cellular atlases, limited integration of spatial and longitudinal data, difficulties in cross-scale modeling, and the lack of robust validation frameworks. Addressing these obstacles requires embedding biological priors into foundation models, developing multi-modal integration strategies, and adopting graph-based and hybrid mechanistic-statistical approaches. The emergence of digital twins--organ- or patient-specific replicas--illustrates how virtual organ models can inform drug discovery, predict toxicity, and guide precision medicine. Ultimately, the trajectory from virtual cells to virtual organs points toward the vision of a virtual human, enabling in silico experimentation at scale. Realizing this goal will demand not only technical breakthroughs but also collaborative validation, ensuring that medical AI navigates these deep waters toward safe and transformative clinical application.","doi":"10.71373/iqha9494","year":"2024","venue":"iCell","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001659","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_component_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract reads as a forward-looking commentary on virtual cells, organs, and foundation models rather than a primary model paper. It does not clearly specify a substantive text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"“Navigating the Deep Waters of Medical AI Models” and “Ultimately, the trajectory...”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001659","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"This reads as an editorial perspective on virtual cells, virtual organs, and foundation models, not a primary model paper. It mentions generative simulations and foundation models, but gives no concrete model architecture or training evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"predictive and generative simulations\" and \"embedding biological priors into foundation models\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001663","title":"Direct-Coding DNA With Multilevel Parallelism","abstract":"The cost and time to sequence entire genomes have been on a steady and rapid decline since the early 2000s, leading to an explosion of genomic data. In contrast, the growth rates for digital storage device capacity, CPU clock speed, and networking bandwidth have been much more moderate. This gap means that the need for storing, transmitting, and processing sequenced genomic data is outpacing the capacities of the underlying technologies. Compounding the problem is the fact that traditional data compression techniques used for natural language or images are not optimal for genomic data. To address this challenge, many data-compression techniques have been developed, offering a range of tradeoffs between compression ratio, computation time, memory requirements, and complexity. This paper focuses on a specific technique on one extreme of this tradeoff, namely two-bit coding, wherein every base in a genomic sequence is compressed from its original 8-bit ASCII representation to a unique two-bit binary representation. Even for this simple direct-coding scheme, current implementations leave room for significant performance improvements. Here, we show that this encoding can exploit multiple levels of parallelism in modern computer architectures to maximize encoding and decoding efficiency. Our open-source implementation achieves encoding and decoding rates of billions of bases per second, which are much higher than previously reported results. In fact, our measured throughput is typically limited only by the speed of the underlying storage media.","doi":"10.1109/LCA.2024.3355109","year":"2024","venue":"IEEE computer architecture letters","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001663","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a genomic data compression/encoding paper, not a generative text-bio model paper. The abstract describes direct coding of DNA bases and does not mention any text or language component.","evidence_snippet":"“two-bit coding” for “genomic sequence” compression"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001663","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper is about DNA direct-coding and parallelized compression, which is not a generative text-bio model. The abstract focuses on encoding and decoding throughput rather than any model pretraining or transferable representation learning.","evidence_snippet":"\"two-bit coding\" and \"encoding and decoding efficiency\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001665","title":"Memo-Patho: Bridging Local-Global Transmembrane Protein Contexts with Contrastive Pretraining for Alignment-Free Pathogenicity Prediction","abstract":"Abstract\n                Understanding the pathogenic impact of protein mutations remains a fundamental challenge in genomic medicine, particularly for transmembrane proteins (TMPs), a functionally critical yet structurally under-annotated class that includes numerous drug targets. Current variant effect predictors often fall short in this domain due to computational burdens, reliance on multiple sequence alignments (MSAs), and limited ability to generalize to TMP-specific constraints. Here we present Memo-Patho, an alignment-free deep learning framework specifically optimized for TMP mutation pathogenicity prediction. Memo-Patho integrates global and local representations from protein language models (PLMs) and predicted structural features. Furthermore, it introduces a contrastive pre-training strategy that learns discriminative features by comparing benign and pathogenic mutations within the same protein backbone. This design enables the model to capture functional disruptions in challenging contexts without structural or evolutionary alignments. Memo-Patho outperforms state-of-the-art predictors on two types of TMP benchmark datasets (accuracy up to 0.93) and unseen-protein generalization tasks, showing strong agreement with evolutionary conservation profiles. Its predictions are further validated on a manually curated KCNQ1 dataset of 55 ion channel variants, achieving an accuracy of 0.84 and outperforming existing tools. The model also supports high-throughput scanning, efficiently analyzing over 15000 variants in under 80 minutes. Our results establish Memo-Patho as a fast, generalizable, and biologically grounded approach for variant effect prediction in TMPs. Its alignment-free design, contrastive learning paradigm, and clinical robustness mark a step forward towards scalable interpretation of TMP variation for both basic research and precision medicine applications.","doi":"10.1101/2025.05.18.654712","year":"2025","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001665","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Uses protein language model representations as features for TMP pathogenicity prediction rather than presenting a substantive text-bio model. The abstract describes a downstream prediction framework, not a text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"integrates global and local representations from protein language models (PLMs)"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001665","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a contrastive pretraining framework for TMP pathogenicity prediction, not a generative architecture. It uses protein language model features but does not show foundation-model-like broad pretraining or broad applicability.","evidence_snippet":"contrastive pre-training strategy ... for TMP mutation pathogenicity prediction"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001666","title":"Abstract 6339: Deep plasma proteomics at scale enabling proteogenomic analyses in a lung cancer (NSCLC) study","abstract":"Our ~20,000 genes encode over one million protein variants, given alternative splice forms, allelic variation, and protein modification. Though large-scale genomics studies have expanded our understanding of biology, similarly, scaled deep and untargeted proteomics studies of biofluids have remained impractical due to complex workflows. To address this need, we have previously described Proteograph࣪, a novel platform that leverages the protein-corona interactions of nanoparticles for deep and untargeted proteomic sampling at scale.\n Using Proteograph࣪ in a non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) cohort, we previously conducted a deep interrogation of plasma from 141 subjects: 61 early-stage NSCLC subjects and 80 non-cancer controls. We identified 2,499 plasma proteins, with 1,992 present in ≥ 25% of the samples. Leveraging this data, we created a biomarker classifier distinguishing NSCLC from controls with area under the receiver operating characteristic curve of 0.911. In this study, we now re-analyze the data with the more sensitive DIA-NN software to enhance protein depth while preserving the accuracy of the classifier. In addition, to show the added value of our platform in combination with genomic data, we integrate previously sequenced exome data with our proteomic data to build a multi-modal proteogenomic deep learning classifier. Our results outline proteogenomic workflows for robust biomarker discovery and cohort subtyping.\n The Proteograph࣪ platform interrogates the plasma proteome at previously impractical combinations of scale, depth and coverage, and enables the development of improved classification models and the study of proteogenomics.\n 1Blume et al. Nat. Comm. (2020)\n Citation Format: Mahdi Zamanighomi, Harendra Guturu, Jian Wang, Amir Alavi, Tristan Brown, Daniel Hornburg, Moaraj Hasan, Shadi Ferdosi, Khatereh Motamedchaboki, Margaret Donovan, Theodore Platt, Ryan Benz, Asim Siddiqui, Serafim Batzoglou. Deep plasma proteomics at scale enabling proteogenomic analyses in a lung cancer (NSCLC) study [abstract]. In: Proceedings of the American Association for Cancer Research Annual Meeting 2022; 2022 Apr 8-13. Philadelphia (PA): AACR; Cancer Res 2022;82(12_Suppl):Abstract nr 6339.","doi":"10.1158/1538-7445.am2022-6339","year":"2022","venue":"Cancer Research","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001666","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a proteomics/proteogenomic classifier built from plasma proteins and exome data. No text or language component is described in the abstract.","evidence_snippet":"build a multi-modal proteogenomic deep learning classifier"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001666","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a proteomics/proteogenomic classifier and software re-analysis, not a generative model paper. The abstract focuses on biomarker classification from plasma proteins and exome data.","evidence_snippet":"build a multi-modal proteogenomic deep learning classifier"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0026.stderr.log:2760:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/adjudicator.jsonl:68:{"record_id": "rec_000249", "paper_type": "application_wrapper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "generative_model_present": "yes", "foundation_model_evidence": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "decision_rationale": "Although the abstract uses scLLMs, the contribution is a parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework around an existing backbone, not a new foundation model. The reviewer conflict is resolved conservatively as an application wrapper.", "evidence_snippet": "\"single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework\" and \"freezing the backbone model\""}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0026.stderr.log:2936:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0012.prompt.txt:52:[{"record_id":"rec_000240","title":"Agentic Authoring of Interactive Multiview Visualizations in Genomics","abstract":"Diverse genomics data, scientific questions, and analysis tasks typically demand highly specialized visualizations. Therefore, users often must customize or author new ones tailored to their data. Existing tools are usually either limited in customization or require substantial learning or programming, and even expressive tools assume visualization expertise many users lack. Agentic and large language model (LLM) approaches are increasingly applied to complex scientific tasks, including visualization. Natural-language conversational interfaces offer a promising path to democratizing the authoring of complex visualizations. In the context of genomics, these approaches face additional challenges: genomics visualizations typically integrate heterogeneous data types and are composed of multiple linked interactive views. These challenges motivate more structured LLM-based schemes. We first characterize where vanilla LLM generation succeeds and fails for genomics visualization, identifying eight quality dimensions. We then compare six schemes--direct generation, a fixed pipeline, and four agentic configurations varying in the number of specialist agents and the presence of a reviewer--across 159 cases spanning three levels of query ambiguity and specification complexity. All schemes use the Gosling visualization grammar as structured output. Agentic iteration substantially improves perceived quality over both baselines, while more complex agent architectures yield no additional benefit. We discuss implications for designing agentic systems for domain-specific visualization authoring. All supplemental materials are available at https://osf.io/uqe83.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["arxiv"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000240","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The work studies agentic LLM schemes for authoring genomics visualizations, not a candidate bio-foundational model. The LLM is used as a tooling layer for visualization generation.","evidence_snippet":"Agentic and large language model (LLM) approaches are increasingly applied to complex scientific tasks, including visualization"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000240","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper studies LLM-based generation of visualization grammars, so a generative workflow is present. But it is an agentic authoring/application paper, and the abstract does not establish foundation-model-style pretraining for a candidate text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“vanilla LLM generation” and “All schemes use the Gosling visualization grammar as structured output”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000249","title":"Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT","abstract":"Abstract\n                Single-cell large language models (scLLMs) capture essential biological insights from vast single-cell atlases but struggle in out-of-context applications, where zero-shot predictions can be unreliable. To address this, we introduce a single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework that integrates learnable, low-dimensional adapters into scLLMs. By freezing the backbone model and updating only the adapter parameters, scPEFT efficiently adapts to specific tasks using limited custom data. This approach mitigates catastrophic forgetting, reduces parameter tuning by over 96%, and decreases GPU memory usage by more than half, significantly enhancing scLLMs’s accessibility for resource-constrained researchers. Validated across diverse datasets, scPEFT outperformed zero-shot models and traditional fine-tuning in disease-specific, cross-species, and under-characterized cell population tasks. Its attention-mechanism analysis identified COVID-related genes associated with specific cell states and uncovered unique blood cell subpopulations, demonstrating scPEFT’s capacity for condition-specific interpretations. These findings position scPEFT as an efficient solution for improving scLLMs’ utilities in general single-cell analyses.","doi":"10.1101/2025.04.21.649754","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Machine …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000249","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Introduces scPEFT as a new framework for single-cell large language models with learnable adapters. The model is explicitly for single-cell biological data and fine-tunes scLLMs for downstream tasks.","evidence_snippet":"\"single-cell large language models (scLLMs)\" and \"single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000249","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes parameter-efficient fine-tuning of existing scLLMs rather than a new generative model. It mentions LLMs, but the candidate here is a tuning framework, not a foundation model itself.","evidence_snippet":"“parameter-efficient fine-tuning ... integrates learnable, low-dimensional adapters into scLLMs”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000250","title":"scpFormer: A Foundation Model for Unified Representation and Integration of the Single-Cell Proteomics","abstract":"The integration of single-cell proteomic data is often hindered by the fragmented nature of   , a transformer-based foundation model designed for single-cell proteomics. Pre-trained on","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"arXiv preprint arXiv …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000250","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Presents a transformer-based foundation model for single-cell proteomics. The abstract does not describe any substantive text or language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"a transformer-based foundation model designed for single-cell proteomics\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000250","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_r