tions it is pre-trained on a massive collection of gene sets. The provided text does not clearly describe a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"“gene set foundation model pre-trained on a massive collection of diverse gene sets”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000254","title":"ChromFound: Towards A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data","abstract":"spatial and temporal gene expression patterns [31]. Identifying OCRs at singlecell level is   -scale pretrained deep language model for cell type annotation of single-cell rna-seq data.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Advances in …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000254","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"ChromFound is presented as a universal foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data. The record supports a biological modality but not a text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000254","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title presents ChromFound as a universal foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data. The abstract snippet does not give enough mechanism detail to confirm a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"“Towards A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000256","title":"AICellType: a large language model-based platform for accurate cell type annotation","abstract":"Abstract\n                  Accurate cell type annotation is critical for studying cellular heterogeneity in single-cell and spatial transcriptomics. However, existing methods largely rely on static gene markers, limiting adaptability to diverse biological contexts and data types. To overcome this limitation, we systematically benchmarked 79 large language models (LLMs) over 1130 single-cell and spatial transcriptomics datasets using an evaluation framework combining ontology structure and semantic reasoning to quantify model performance in biological relevance and annotation robustness. Claude 3.5 Sonnet achieved the best overall performance, balancing weighted accuracy (76%), robustness, inference speed, and cost-efficiency. Based on these findings, we developed AICellType (https://AICellType.jinlab.online), a free, open-source R package and web platform that integrates seamlessly with Seurat workflows, supports multiple species and tissues, and enables flexible model deployment via OpenRouter or custom APIs. By leveraging LLMs’ capacity to interpret marker–cell type associations, AICellType provides a scalable, efficient, and accessible solution for real-world cell annotation in both single-cell and spatial omics research.","doi":"10.1093/bib/bbag151","year":"2026","venue":"Briefings in …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000256","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract centers on benchmarking 79 large language models across 1130 transcriptomics datasets and releasing a platform. This is primarily a benchmark/resource and application platform paper, not a new candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically benchmarked 79 large language models\" and \"developed AICellType ... a free, open-source R package and web platform\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000256","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a benchmarking study over 79 LLMs and a deployed platform for cell type annotation. It does not present a new generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“we systematically benchmarked 79 large language models” and “developed AICellType ... a ... platform”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0026.stderr.log:2968:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0012.stderr.log:65:[{"record_id":"rec_000240","title":"Agentic Authoring of Interactive Multiview Visualizations in Genomics","abstract":"Diverse genomics data, scientific questions, and analysis tasks typically demand highly specialized visualizations. Therefore, users often must customize or author new ones tailored to their data. Existing tools are usually either limited in customization or require substantial learning or programming, and even expressive tools assume visualization expertise many users lack. Agentic and large language model (LLM) approaches are increasingly applied to complex scientific tasks, including visualization. Natural-language conversational interfaces offer a promising path to democratizing the authoring of complex visualizations. In the context of genomics, these approaches face additional challenges: genomics visualizations typically integrate heterogeneous data types and are composed of multiple linked interactive views. These challenges motivate more structured LLM-based schemes. We first characterize where vanilla LLM generation succeeds and fails for genomics visualization, identifying eight quality dimensions. We then compare six schemes--direct generation, a fixed pipeline, and four agentic configurations varying in the number of specialist agents and the presence of a reviewer--across 159 cases spanning three levels of query ambiguity and specification complexity. All schemes use the Gosling visualization grammar as structured output. Agentic iteration substantially improves perceived quality over both baselines, while more complex agent architectures yield no additional benefit. We discuss implications for designing agentic systems for domain-specific visualization authoring. All supplemental materials are available at https://osf.io/uqe83.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["arxiv"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000240","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The work studies agentic LLM schemes for authoring genomics visualizations, not a candidate bio-foundational model. The LLM is used as a tooling layer for visualization generation.","evidence_snippet":"Agentic and large language model (LLM) approaches are increasingly applied to complex scientific tasks, including visualization"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000240","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper studies LLM-based generation of visualization grammars, so a generative workflow is present. But it is an agentic authoring/application paper, and the abstract does not establish foundation-model-style pretraining for a candidate text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“vanilla LLM generation” and “All schemes use the Gosling visualization grammar as structured output”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000249","title":"Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT","abstract":"Abstract\n                Single-cell large language models (scLLMs) capture essential biological insights from vast single-cell atlases but struggle in out-of-context applications, where zero-shot predictions can be unreliable. To address this, we introduce a single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework that integrates learnable, low-dimensional adapters into scLLMs. By freezing the backbone model and updating only the adapter parameters, scPEFT efficiently adapts to specific tasks using limited custom data. This approach mitigates catastrophic forgetting, reduces parameter tuning by over 96%, and decreases GPU memory usage by more than half, significantly enhancing scLLMs’s accessibility for resource-constrained researchers. Validated across diverse datasets, scPEFT outperformed zero-shot models and traditional fine-tuning in disease-specific, cross-species, and under-characterized cell population tasks. Its attention-mechanism analysis identified COVID-related genes associated with specific cell states and uncovered unique blood cell subpopulations, demonstrating scPEFT’s capacity for condition-specific interpretations. These findings position scPEFT as an efficient solution for improving scLLMs’ utilities in general single-cell analyses.","doi":"10.1101/2025.04.21.649754","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Machine …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000249","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Introduces scPEFT as a new framework for single-cell large language models with learnable adapters. The model is explicitly for single-cell biological data and fine-tunes scLLMs for downstream tasks.","evidence_snippet":"\"single-cell large language models (scLLMs)\" and \"single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000249","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"