./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0482.response.txt:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_003849","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"A unified zero-shot inference framework for existing single-cell foundation models, not a new candidate model. The abstract focuses on environment isolation, preprocessing, tokenization, and benchmarking of model embeddings.","evidence_snippet":"“unified zero-shot inference framework for single-cell foundation models” and “manages environment isolation, preprocessing, and tokenization”"},{"record_id":"rec_003850","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an evaluation study of existing single-cell large language models across tasks, rather than a new model paper. It benchmarks Geneformer and scGPT on perturbation prediction and cell type annotation.","evidence_snippet":"“systematically evaluate two representative scFMs, Geneformer and scGPT” across tasks"},{"record_id":"rec_003851","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a manually annotated text corpus and text mining utilities, not a new generative model. It links literature mentions of cell phenotypes to Cell Ontology terms.","evidence_snippet":"“manually annotated collection” and “fine-tuning transformer-based models on CellLink”"},{"record_id":"rec_003852","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title presents a generative foundation model for multimodal histopathology, and the abstract mentions natural language prompts. This supports a substantive bio-plus-text model rather than a wrapper.","evidence_snippet":"“A Generative Foundation Model for Multimodal Histopathology” and “natural language prompts”"},{"record_id":"rec_003853","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title suggests an AI systems paper, but the provided abstract text is too fragmentary to establish whether it is a primary model paper or a review-style piece. The text-bio modeling details are not clear from the snippet.","evidence_snippet":"“From Modality Translation to Regulatory Reasoning: Interpretable AI for Single-Cell Multi-Omic Systems” with only fragmentary abstract text"},{"record_id":"rec_003854","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary spatial multi-modal omics method paper, but the abstract contains no substantive text or language component. It focuses on gene expression, protein abundance, chromatin accessibility, and graph learning.","evidence_snippet":"“Spatial multi-modal omics technologies” and “adaptive graph learning for spatial multi-modal omics analysis”"},{"record_id":"rec_003855","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The venue and framing indicate a review article on a broad topic rather than a primary model paper. The abstract fragment is about advancing single-cell omics and therapeutics with quantum computing.","evidence_snippet":"Title/venue framing: “Nature Reviews Molecular …” and “Advancing single-cell omics and cell-based therapeutics with quantum computing”"},{"record_id":"rec_003856","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title describes a biologically informed dual-branch transformer for drug-induced cellular perturbation responses, which is a primary model paper. The abstract fragment also references single-cell large language model architectures and NLP-style tokens.","evidence_snippet":"“biologically informed dual-branch transformer” and “single-cell large language models” / “word tokens in natural language processing”"}]}
./data/dedup_update_2026-07-06/deduplicated_records.json:1904:      "abstract": "In single-cell (sc)-based scientific discovery, text-formatted biomedical prior knowledge and signaling graphs are essential for annotating and interpreting numeric sc-omics data and for generating novel testable hypotheses. A major limitation of existing single-cell large language models (scLLMs) is that they rely on numeric expression data with gene names as the only textual signal, while comprehensive biomedical priors — cellular localization, gene function, disease associations, and signaling interaction patterns — remain absent from the model input. We introduce  CellTosg2Sequence , a textual-prior- and signaling-graph-augmented cell-omics-sentence language model.  A lightweight heterogeneous graph encoder maps a curated 62,507-node biomedical knowledge graph (KG) into compact virtual tokens that are prepended to each cell sentence, allowing the language model to condition on biological structure with minimal sequence-length overhead. We train CellTosg2Sequence with a three-stage objective: Stage I anchors the KG channel under autoregressive language-model pretraining, leveraging Qwen2.5-32B’s own language reasoning for rapid KG alignment; Stage II aligns labels via supervised fine-tuning with KG-anchored InfoNCE; Stage III applies Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with an ontology-hierarchy reward, enabling  free-generation cell-type prediction that generalizes beyond the closed training vocabulary.  Across multiple benchmarks and ablation experiments, CellTosg2Sequence outperforms strong baselines. All results are achieved with lightweight LoRA training and a single unified checkpoint. <h4>Data ethics</h4>  This work uses publicly available single-cell datasets from the Human Cell Atlas (  https://www.humancellatlas.org ) and the Tahoe-100M consortium. All HCA constituent studies were collected under appropriate donor consent and institutional oversight as described in their original publications; we perform computational re-analysis only and introduce no new human subjects data. HCA data access follows the HCA Data Portal terms of use. No new patient data are collected in this study; no additional IRB approval is required for this secondary computational analysis.",
