onent_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This presents a new generative self-expression model for single-cell and spatial multimodal data. The abstract describes omics integration and representation learning, but no substantive text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"“we propose a contrastive and generative deep self-expression model” for “single-cell and spatial multimodal data”"},{"record_id":"rec_001661","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The work reuses pre-trained DNA language models and compares embedding-based pipelines versus fine-tuning for downstream genomic prediction. That is an application of existing models, not a new substantive text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“Large pre-trained DNA language models” and “embedding-based pipelines”"},{"record_id":"rec_001662","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"FEDKEA integrates a pretrained protein language model with KNN for enzyme annotation, which is a downstream application pipeline around existing model embeddings. The abstract does not support a new text-bio foundation model with a substantive bridge.","evidence_snippet":"“integrates ESM-2 and distance-weighted KNN” for “enzyme function annotation”"},{"record_id":"rec_001663","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a genomic data compression/encoding paper, not a generative text-bio model paper. The abstract describes direct coding of DNA bases and does not mention any text or language component.","evidence_snippet":"“two-bit coding” for “genomic sequence” compression"},{"record_id":"rec_001664","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says this review synthesizes the technology and applications of droplet-based scRNA-seq. It is a review, not a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"“This review synthesizes the technological evolution” of droplet-based single-cell RNA sequencing"}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0065.stdout.log:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_001345","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes MERFISH/scRNA analysis with a conditional variational autoencoder, but no substantive text or language component is described. This is a biological generative model paper, not a text-bio foundation model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"multimodal single-cell and spatial transcriptomics with generative AI\"; \"adapted the conditional variational autoencoder ENVI\""},{"record_id":"rec_001348","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly frames the article as a review of foundation models in pathology rather than a primary model report. It discusses multimodal pathology-text models in review context, but does not describe a specific candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"Foundation models are revolutionising pathology\"; \"visual-language models such as CONCH integrate histopathological images with biomedical text\""},{"record_id":"rec_001356","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"SeqMate uses an existing LLM to automate RNA-seq preparation, analysis, and reporting. The abstract presents a workflow wrapper around the model rather than a substantive text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"utilizing the power of a large language model (LLM) to automate both data preparation and analysis\"; \"producing written reports\""},{"record_id":"rec_001357","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a whole-genome sequencing and gene-annotation study of a bacterial isolate. No text or language component is described, and it is not clearly a model paper type.","evidence_snippet":"\"Whole Genome Sequencing of Enterococcus faecalis\"; \"gene annotation\""},{"record_id":"rec_001358","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract presents a new parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework for single-cell large language models. It clearly links a language-model backbone to biological single-cell tasks and downstream transfer settings.","evidence_snippet":"\"single-cell large language models (scLLMs)\"; \"freezing the backbone model and updating only the adapter parameters\""},{"record_id":"rec_001359","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"NicheTrans is a biological cross-omics translation framework, but the abstract does not describe any text or language component. The model is transformer-based, but generative behavior is not explicitly stated.","evidence_snippet":"\"spatially-aware cross-omics translation method\"; \"Transformer-based multi-modal deep learning framework\""}]}
./data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_09/run_summary.json:6230:            "text": "Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) profiles gene expression at the level of individual cells, providing a fine-grained view of cellular heterogeneity [1-3]. The complexity and high dimensionality of scRNA-seq data necessitate powerful computational approaches that can leverage massive datasets efficiently and accurately [4]. Inspired by the success of large language models (LLMs) in natural language processing [5-7], specialized single-cell large language models (scLLMs) have emerged, leveraging self-supervised pre-training on extensive expression datasets to produce robust cell representations for downstream tasks such as cell annotation and clustering [8-11]. However, existing scLLMs are mostly pre-trained solely on scRNA-seq data, inherently constraining the breadth of their cell representations.\n\nIn this work, we explore language-enhanced single-cell representation learning , aiming to integrate the fine-grained molecular signals from scRNA-seq with the high-level biological knowledge encoded in textual descriptions and metadata. As shown in Figure 1, textual descriptions encode contextual information-such as species, tissue origin, and cell type-that is not directly captured by expression profiles but is critical for downstream biological interpretation. Incorporating such textual knowledge offers a promising path toward more comprehensive and semantically meaningful cell representations.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nThe scRNA-seq Data of a cell\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nThe Textual Description of a cell\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 2: Cell type annotation results with different cell representation methods. (a) Cell type annotation accuracies on the full dataset and test set. Using cell sentences as cell representation leads to significant accuracy degradation. (b-d) UMAP visualization of classification results and the ground truth. Classification using cell sentences yields a lower accuracy score and exhibits poorer recognition capabilities in certain cell clusters.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 1: Comparison between the scRNA-seq results and the textual descriptions of a cell.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nTo harness the rich biological knowledge in text descriptions for single-cell analysis, recent studies have explored joint cell-text modeling [12-15]. However, we find that these efforts often overlook key aspects of language-enhanced cell representation learning:\n\n- Insufficient Unimodal Cell Representation Learning. Robust unimodal cell representation learning is the foundation before incorporating language enhancement. However, many existing works rely on general-purpose text LLMs or randomly initialized cell encoders, lacking the critical biological prior learned from large-scale scRNA-seq data for single-cell analysis [15, 16, 13]. Other methods represent cells using only the top 30-100 expressed genes [17, 18], or discard critical quantitative expression information and low-abundance genes [8, 19, 12]. They result in substantial information loss and reduced annotation accuracy ( cf. Figure 2).\n- Incomplete Cell-Text Alignment. Effectively leveraging textual knowledge requires comprehensive cross-modal alignment. Current cell-text models mainly focus on the development of text generation ability with generative objectives to support more flexible human interaction [17, 15, 19]. Nevertheless, real-world scientific discovery poses more demands on high-quality cell representations that can support more precise cell type annotation and clustering. To improve the performance on these crucial tasks, we also emphasize the importance of discrimination objectives, which help the model distinguish correct cell-type labels for more accurate cell annotation.\n\nTo address these gaps, we propose scMMGPT ( S ingleC ell M ulti M odal G enerative P re-trained T ransformer), a language-enhanced cell representation learning framework designed for single-cell transcriptomic analysis. Rather than exploring chat-based interfaces with text LLMs [15, 17, 19], scMMGPT emphasizes improved performance in essential single-cell analysis tasks with information from the text modality. The overall architecture of scMMGPT is shown in Figure 3. To ensure robust cell representation learning , scMMGPT preserves critical quantitative gene expression information in the tokenization processes. Furthermore, we construct comprehensive pre-training datasets comprising 27 million cells with scRNA-seq data from CellxGene [20] and textual descriptions from Wikipedia and OBO Foundry [21], guaranteeing rich biological prior.\n\nTo achieve comprehensive cell-text alignment , scMMGPT incorporates two separate projectors for the bidirectional information sharing between scLLM and text LLM, and a comprehensive two-stage pre-training strategy with both discriminative and generative objectives. The discriminative stage first aligns cell and text representations into a joint latent space and establishes cell annotation capability by training the model to distinguish the correct text description given scRNA-seq data. Concurrently, the generative stage promotes semantic alignment by reconstructing textual descriptions from cell embeddings and vice versa.\n\nTable 1: Comparison of unimodal scLLMs and cell-text multimodal LLMs.\n\n|                    |                      | Cell Representation Learning   | Cell Representation Learning   | Cell-Text Alignment Strategies   | Cell-Text Alignment Strategies   |\n|--------------------|----------------------|--------------------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| Model              | w/ Textual Knowledge | Strong Bio. Insights           | No Info. Loss                  | Discriminative                   | Generative                       |\n| scGPT [9]          | ×                    | ✓                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ×                                |\n| scFoundation [11]  | ×                    | ✓                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ×                                |\n| scMulan [14]       | ×                    | ✓                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ✓                                |\n| GenePT [16]        | ✓                    | ×                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ×                                |\n| LangCell [12]      | ✓                    | ✓                              | ×                              | ✓                                | ×                                |\n| C2S [17]           | ✓                    | ×                              | ×                              | ×                                | ✓                                |\n| CellWhisperer [19] | ✓                    | ✓                              | ×                              | ✓                                | ✓                                |\n| InstructCell [15]  | ✓                    | ×                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ✓                                |\n| scMMGPT (Ours)     | ✓                    | ✓                              | ✓                              | ✓                                | ✓                                |\n\nWith these breakthroughs, scMMGPT significantly surpasses existing unimodal and multimodal methods, establishing a new standard for leveraging textual knowledge in single-cell transcriptomics analysis. Our method achieves steady improvement in cell type annotation with approximately 10% improved F1 scores across all benchmarks (§4.1.1) and much better accuracies under out-ofdistribution settings (§4.3). It generates cell representations with higher biological signals and less batch effect on both common cells and disease cells (§4.1.2). The cell description generated also contains more accurate cell type information (§4.1.3). Comprehensive ablation studies further validate the effectiveness of the key components (§4.2).",
./data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_09/run_summary.json:7386:          "text": "Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) profiles gene expression at the level of individual cells, providing a fine-grained view of cellular heterogeneity [1-3]. The complexity and high dimensionality of scRNA-seq data necessitate powerful computational approaches that can leverage massive datasets efficiently and accurately [4]. Inspired by the success of large language models (LLMs) in natural language processing [5-7], specialized single-cell large language models (scLLMs) have emerged, leveraging self-supervised pre-training on extensive expression datasets to produce robust cell representations for downstream tasks such as cell annotation and clustering [8-11]. However, existing scLLMs are mostly pre-trained solely on scRNA-seq data, inherently constraining the breadth of their cell representations.\n\nIn this work, we explore language-enhanced single-cell representation learning , aiming to integrate the fine-grained molecular signals from scRNA-seq with the high-level biological knowledge encoded in textual descriptions and metadata. As shown in Figure 1, textual descriptions encode contextual information-such as species, tissue origin, and cell type-that is not directly captured by expression profiles but is critical for downstream biological interpretation. Incorporating such textual knowledge offers a promising path toward more comprehensive and semantically meaningful cell representations.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nThe scRNA-seq Data of a cell\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nThe Textual Description of a cell\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 2: Cell type annotation results with different cell representation methods. (a) Cell type annotation accuracies on the full dataset and test set. Using cell sentences as cell representation leads to significant accuracy degradation. (b-d) UMAP visualization of classification results and the ground truth. Classification using cell sentences yields a lower accuracy score and exhibits poorer recognition capabilities in certain cell clusters.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 1: Comparison between the scRNA-seq results and the textual descriptions of a cell.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nTo harness the rich biological knowledge in text descriptions for single-cell analysis, recent studies have explored joint cell-text modeling [12-15]. However, we find that these efforts often overlook key aspects of language-enhanced cell representation learning:\n\n- Insufficient Unimodal Cell Representation Learning. Robust unimodal cell representation learning is the foundation before incorporating language enhancement. However, many existing works rely on general-purpose text LLMs or randomly initialized cell encoders, lacking the critical biological prior learned from large-scale scRNA-seq data for single-cell analysis [15, 16, 13]. Other methods represent cells using only the top 30-100 expressed genes [17, 18], or discard critical quantitative expression information and low-abundance genes [8, 19, 12]. They result in substantial information loss and reduced annotation accuracy ( cf. Figure 2).\n- Incomplete Cell-Text Alignment. Effectively leveraging textual knowledge requires comprehensive cross-modal alignment. Current cell-text models mainly focus on the development of text generation ability with generative objectives to support more flexible human interaction [17, 15, 19]. Nevertheless, real-world scientific discovery poses more demands on high-quality cell representations that can support more precise cell type annotation and clustering. To improve the performance on these crucial tasks, we also emphasize the importance of discrimination objectives, which help the model distinguish correct cell-type labels for more accurate cell annotation.\n\nTo address these gaps, we propose scMMGPT ( S ingleC ell M ulti M odal G enerative P re-trained T ransformer), a language-enhanced cell representation learning framework designed for single-cell transcriptomic analysis. Rather than exploring chat-based interfaces with text LLMs [15, 17, 19], scMMGPT emphasizes improved performance in essential single-cell analysis tasks with information from the text modality. The overall architecture of scMMGPT is shown in Figure 3. To ensure robust cell representation learning , scMMGPT preserves critical quantitative gene expression information in the tokenization processes. Furthermore, we construct comprehensive pre-training datasets comprising 27 million cells with scRNA-seq data from CellxGene [20] and textual descriptions from Wikipedia and OBO Foundry [21], guaranteeing rich biological prior.\n\nTo achieve comprehensive cell-text alignment , scMMGPT incorporates two separate projectors for the bidirectional information sharing between scLLM and text LLM, and a comprehensive two-stage pre-training strategy with both discriminative and generative objectives. The discriminative stage first aligns cell and text representations into a joint latent space and establishes cell annotation capability by training the model to distinguish the correct text description given scRNA-seq data. Concurrently, the generative stage promotes semantic alignment by reconstructing textual descriptions from cell embeddings and vice versa.\n\nTable 1: Comparison of unimodal scLLMs and cell-text multimodal LLMs.\n\n|                    |                      | Cell Representation Learning   | Cell Representation Learning   | Cell-Text Alignment Strategies   | Cell-Text Alignment Strategies   |\n|--------------------|----------------------|--------------------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| Model              | w/ Textual Knowledge | Strong Bio. Insights           | No Info. Loss                  | Discriminative                   | Generative                       |\n| scGPT [9]          | ×                    | ✓                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ×                                |\n| scFoundation [11]  | ×                    | ✓                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ×                                |\n| scMulan [14]       | ×                    | ✓                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ✓                                |\n| GenePT [16]        | ✓                    | ×                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ×                                |\n| LangCell [12]      | ✓                    | ✓                              | ×                              | ✓                                | ×                                |\n| C2S [17]           | ✓                    | ×                              | ×                              | ×                                | ✓                                |\n| CellWhisperer [19] | ✓                    | ✓                              | ×                              | ✓                                | ✓                                |\n| InstructCell [15]  | ✓                    | ×                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ✓                                |\n| scMMGPT (Ours)     | ✓                    | ✓                              | ✓                              | ✓                                | ✓                                |\n\nWith these breakthroughs, scMMGPT significantly surpasses existing unimodal and multimodal methods, establishing a new standard for leveraging textual knowledge in single-cell transcriptomics analysis. Our method achieves steady improvement in cell type annotation with approximately 10% improved F1 scores across all benchmarks (§4.1.1) and much better accuracies under out-ofdistribution settings (§4.3). It generates cell representations with higher biological signals and less batch effect on both common cells and disease cells (§4.1.2). The cell description generated also contains more accurate cell type information (§4.1.3). Comprehensive ablation studies further validate the effectiveness of the key components (§4.2).",
