undation model”"}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0211.parsed.json:12:    "decision_rationale": "The abstract describes an evaluation of existing single-cell large language models, not a new model paper. It benchmarks Geneformer and scGPT across biological tasks.",
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0211.prompt.txt:52:[{"record_id":"rec_003850","title":"Evaluating the learnability of single-cell large language models on multiple tasks","abstract":"Abstract\n                The rise of single-cell foundation models (scFMs) has sparked interest in their potential to unify diverse biological tasks. However, their practical utility and the validity of scaling laws—the assumption that performance improves with model and data size—remain under-examined. Here, we systematically evaluate two representative scFMs, Geneformer and scGPT, across perturbation prediction and cell type annotation tasks. Our findings suggest that the benefits of large-scale pretraining are strongly task-dependent, conferring substantial advantages in cell type annotation but limited gains in perturbation prediction. Furthermore, our results indicate that increasing model size does not guarantee improved performance and can even be detrimental, challenging the ``bigger is better'' paradigm. By comparing model performance on real versus synthetic data with different levels of complexity, our analysis suggests that for perturbation prediction, the tested scFMs capture little more than simple summary statistics and may struggle to learn complex biological interactions. These results highlight the need to move beyond scaling and toward developing models that integrate deeper biological knowledge. We suggest that a renewed focus on task-specific architectures and biologically-informed priors may be critical for unlocking the true potential of foundation models in single-cell biology.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003850","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an evaluation study of existing single-cell large language models across tasks, rather than a new model paper. It benchmarks Geneformer and scGPT on perturbation prediction and cell type annotation.","evidence_snippet":"“systematically evaluate two representative scFMs, Geneformer and scGPT” across tasks"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003850","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper evaluates existing single-cell large language models rather than introducing a new model. It clearly discusses large-scale pretraining and task transfer, but the abstract does not explicitly establish a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically evaluate two representative scFMs, Geneformer and scGPT\" and \"benefits of large-scale pretraining\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003853","title":"From Modality Translation to Regulatory Reasoning: Interpretable AI for Single-Cell Multi-Omic Systems","abstract":"Building on this foundation, we contribute to population- 42 ix 3.4 Determining cell type–specific  eQTLs from single-cell  commonly utilized in natural language processing models as well","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003853","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title suggests an AI systems paper, but the provided abstract text is too fragmentary to establish whether it is a primary model paper or a review-style piece. The text-bio modeling details are not clear from the snippet.","evidence_snippet":"“From Modality Translation to Regulatory Reasoning: Interpretable AI for Single-Cell Multi-Omic Systems” with only fragmentary abstract text"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003853","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The provided text is too fragmentary to identify the work type or determine whether it describes a generative foundation model. It contains isolated technical phrases but no clear model description.","evidence_snippet":"fragmentary text such as \"From Modality Translation to Regulatory Reasoning\" and \"commonly utilized in natural language processing models\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"none","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003855","title":"Advancing single-cell omics and cell-based therapeutics with quantum computing","abstract":"of single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data, cell-type  have been proposed for  performing natural language  models to complex generative and AI foundation models 125","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Reviews Molecular …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003855","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The venue and framing indicate a review article on a broad topic rather than a primary model paper. The abstract fragment is about advancing single-cell omics and therapeutics with quantum computing.","evidence_snippet":"Title/venue framing: “Nature Reviews Molecular …” and “Advancing single-cell omics and cell-based therapeutics with quantum computing”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003855","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The title indicates a review-style article on single-cell omics and therapeutics rather than a primary model paper. The snippet is too incomplete to establish a specific generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"Advancing single-cell omics and cell-based therapeutics with quantum computing\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003857","title":"AI-powered Immune Cell Knowledge Graph (ICKG) with granular immune contexts enables immune program interpretation","abstract":"Abstract\n                  \n                    The widespread application of single-cell and spatial omics to models and patient samples has transformed immune cell profiling across physiological conditions. However, knowledge of immune cell states, functions, and gene regulation remains fragmented across publications, limiting our ability to synthesize insights and derive mechanistic understanding from the literature. To address this gap and facilitate literature integration, we constructed Immune Cell Knowledge Graphs (ICKGs)—four cell type-specific graphs derived from over 24,000 cancer immunotherapy-focused PubMed abstracts using large language models (LLMs) with “human verifiable” validation. Unlike conventional databases, which provide context-agnostic pathways, ICKGs capture directed, literature-supported relationships among genes, pathways and immune functions, enabling context-aware reasoning. We validated ICKGs using perturbation datasets from cytokine stimulation and CRISPR experiments, demonstrating that ICKGs contain more accurate and immunologically coherent contexts than canonical databases. As a key application, ICKGs provide interpretable and accurate pathway annotations, including signatures unannotated by canonical databases or used in immuno-oncology. To support community use, we created an interactive portal (\n                    https://kchen-lab.github.io/immune-knowledgegraph.github.io/\n                    ) to perform ICKG-based pathway annotations, allowing researchers to explore immune cell-specific insights grounded in literature. This work establishes ICKGs as a scalable framework for immune-specific functional interpretation and mechanistic hypothesis generation in single-cell and spatial omics.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"npj Artificial …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003857","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes construction of Immune Cell Knowledge Graphs from PubMed abstracts with LLMs and an interactive portal. This is a resource/database paper, not a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"constructed Immune Cell Knowledge Graphs (ICKGs)... derived from over 24,000... PubMed abstracts using large language models\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003857","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The work builds Immune Cell Knowledge Graphs using LLMs over PubMed abstracts; it is a literature-integration/annotation system, not a generative model. The abstract does not describe broad pretraining or a foundation-model-like pretrained architecture.","evidence_snippet":"\"constructed Immune Cell Knowledge Graphs... using large language models (LLMs)\" and \"interactive portal ... to perform ICKG-based pathway annotations\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003860","title":"Towards Virtual Cell Construction by AI","abstract":"known biological factors such as cell type, treatment, and tem benchmarking framework  for single-cell disentanglement,  and, where possible, natural language abstractions, I aim to","doi":"","year":"NA","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003860","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The title and fragment read like a perspective or concept piece rather than a concrete model paper. The abstract is too incomplete to establish a substantive text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"Towards Virtual Cell Construction by AI\" and \"I aim to\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003860","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title/abstract fragment is too incomplete to identify the paper type or whether any generative or foundation-model characteristics are present. No clear architecture is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"Towards Virtual Cell Construction by AI\" and a fragmented abstract with no model details"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003863","title":"DestinyNet: A deep-learning framework for cell-fate analysis from lineage-tracing single-cell RNA sequencing data","abstract":"-scSeq datasets to pretrain a foundation model. Such a model, ideally incorporating biological   We used the scRNA-seq GEX information and cell-type annotation curated by CoSpar as","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Patterns","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003863","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"text_component_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper describes a deep-learning framework for cell-fate analysis from lineage-tracing scRNA-seq data and pretraining a foundation model. No substantive text/language component is evident from the abstract fragment.","evidence_snippet":"\"A deep-learning framework for cell-fate analysis from lineage-tracing single-cell RNA sequencing data\" and \"pretrain a foundation model\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003863","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"DestinyNet is described as a deep-learning framework for cell-fate analysis, not a generative architecture. The abstract fragment explicitly mentions pretraining a foundation model, which is evidence of foundation-model-style training.","evidence_snippet":"\"pretrain a foundation model\" and \"deep-learning framework for cell-fate analysis\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0082.prompt.txt:52:[{"record_id":"rec_001656","title":"Machine learning enhances biomarker discovery: From multi- omics to functional genomics.","abstract":"Importance: Biomarkers are critical for precision medicine, supporting disease diagnosis, prognosis, personalized treatments, and monitoring. Traditional biomarker discovery methods, which often focus on single genes or proteins, face several challenges, including limited reproducibility, a limited ability to integrate multiple data streams, high false-positive rates, and inadequate predictive accuracy. Machine learning and deep learning methods, and large language models, paired with advancements in omics technologies, address these limitations by analyzing large, complex multi-omics datasets to identify more reliable and clinically useful biomarkers. Observations: Machine learning and deep learning have proven effective in biomarker discovery by integrating diverse and high-volume data types, such as genomics, transcriptomics, proteomics, metabolomics, imaging, and clinical records. These approaches successfully identify diagnostic, prognostic, and predictive biomarkers across fields, such as oncology, infectious diseases, neurological disorders, and autoimmune diseases. Newer methodological developments include approaches to identify functional biomarkers, notably biosynthetic gene clusters, crucial for discovering antibiotics and anticancer drugs. Key artificial intelligence (AI) techniques include neural networks, transformers, large language models, and feature selection methods, which are finding more and more application to omics data and in clinical settings. However, challenges remain regarding data quality, biological complexity, model interpretability, validation, and generalization. Regulatory and ethical considerations also impact clinical adoption, emphasizing the importance of validated, trustworthy, and explainable AI methods. Conclusions and Relevance: Machine learning, deep learning, and AI agent-based approaches significantly enhance biomarker discovery, providing valuable biological insights and advancing precision medicine. Future research should focus on directly linking genomic data to functional outcomes, particularly with biosynthetic gene clusters and non- coding RNAs. Rigorous validation, model interpretability, and regulatory compliance are essential for clinical implementation. These advancements promise to improve personalized treatment strategies and patient outcomes.","doi":"10.18103/mra.v13i8.6888","year":"2025","venue":"Medical Research Archives","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001656","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a review-style piece on biomarker discovery, summarizing machine learning, deep learning, and LLM applications. The abstract does not describe a single primary text-bio model with its own bridge architecture.","evidence_snippet":"\"Machine learning enhances biomarker discovery: From multi- omics to functional genomics.\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001656","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"This is a review-style article on biomarker discovery rather than a single model study. It discusses transformers and large language models broadly, but does not present a specific generative foundation model for screening.","evidence_snippet":"\"Machine learning, deep learning, and AI agent-based approaches\" and \"Key artificial intelligence techniques include neural networks, transformers, large language models\"."},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001658","title":"Overcoming barriers to the wide adoption of single-cell large language models in biomedical research","abstract":"Transformer-based large language models are gaining traction in biomedical research, particularly in single-cell omics. Despite their promise, the application of single-cell large language models (scLLMs) remains limited in practice. In this Comment, we examine the current landscape of scLLMs and the benchmark studies that assess their applications in various analytical tasks. We highlight existing technical gaps and practical barriers, and discuss future directions toward a more accessible and effective ecosystem to promote the applications of scLLMs in the biomedical community.","doi":"10.1038/s41587-025-02846-y","year":"2025","venue":"Nature Biotechnology","sources":["google_scholar","semantic_scholar","springernature"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001658","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a Comment that discusses the landscape and barriers around single-cell large language models rather than presenting a new model. It mentions biomedical single-cell omics and LLMs, but not a specific primary text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"“In this Comment, we examine the current landscape of scLLMs”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001658","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"This is a Comment on the landscape of single-cell large language models rather than a primary model report. It mentions scLLMs and benchmark studies, but does not describe a specific candidate model or its training.","evidence_snippet":"\"In this Comment, we examine the current landscape of scLLMs\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001659","title":"From Virtual Cell Challenge to Virtual Organs: Navigating the Deep Waters of Medical AI Models","abstract":"Recent advances in single-cell profiling and artificial intelligence have enabled the construction of virtual cells, computational entities that simulate cellular states and responses with unprecedented resolution. Initiatives such as the Virtual Cell World Challenge highlight the potential of large AI models to move beyond annotation toward predictive and generative simulations. Yet, the next frontier lies in scaling from virtual cells to virtual organs, where thousands of cell types interact across spatial, temporal, and biophysical dimensions. This transition exposes major challenges: incomplete cellular atlases, limited integration of spatial and longitudinal data, difficulties in cross-scale modeling, and the lack of robust validation frameworks. Addressing these obstacles requires embedding biological priors into foundation models, developing multi-modal integration strategies, and adopting graph-based and hybrid mechanistic-statistical approaches. The emergence of digital twins--organ- or patient-specific replicas--illustrates how virtual organ models can inform drug discovery, predict toxicity, and guide precision medicine. Ultimately, the trajectory from virtual cells to virtual organs points toward the vision of a virtual human, enabling in silico experimentation at scale. Realizing this goal will demand not only technical breakthroughs but also collaborative validation, ensuring that medical AI navigates these deep waters toward safe and transformative clinical application.","doi":"10.71373/iqha9494","year":"2024","venue":"iCell","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001659","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_component_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract reads as a forward-looking commentary on virtual cells, organs, and foundation models rather than a primary model paper. It does not clearly specify a substantive text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"“Navigating the Deep Waters of Medical AI Models” and “Ultimately, the trajectory...”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001659","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"This reads as an editorial perspective on virtual cells, virtual organs, and foundation models, not a primary model paper. It mentions generative simulations and foundation models, but gives no concrete model architecture or training evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"predictive and generative simulations\" and \"embedding biological priors into foundation models\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001663","title":"Direct-Coding DNA With Multilevel Parallelism","abstract":"The cost and time to sequence entire genomes have been on a steady and rapid decline since the early 2000s, leading to an explosion of genomic data. In contrast, the growth rates for digital storage device capacity, CPU clock speed, and networking bandwidth have been much more moderate. This gap means that the need for storing, transmitting, and processing sequenced genomic data is outpacing the capacities of the underlying technologies. Compounding the problem is the fact that traditional data compression techniques used for natural language or images are not optimal for genomic data. To address this challenge, many data-compression techniques have been developed, offering a range of tradeoffs between compression ratio, computation time, memory requirements, and complexity. This paper focuses on a specific technique on one extreme of this tradeoff, namely two-bit coding, wherein every base in a genomic sequence is compressed from its original 8-bit ASCII representation to a unique two-bit binary representation. Even for this simple direct-coding scheme, current implementations leave room for significant performance improvements. Here, we show that this encoding can exploit multiple levels of parallelism in modern computer architectures to maximize encoding and decoding efficiency. Our open-source implementation achieves encoding and decoding rates of billions of bases per second, which are much higher than previously reported results. In fact, our measured throughput is typically limited only by the speed of the underlying storage media.","doi":"10.1109/LCA.2024.3355109","year":"2024","venue":"IEEE computer architecture letters","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001663","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a genomic data compression/encoding paper, not a generative text-bio model paper. The abstract describes direct coding of DNA bases and does not mention any text or language component.","evidence_snippet":"“two-bit coding” for “genomic sequence” compression"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001663","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper is about DNA direct-coding and parallelized compression, which is not a generative text-bio model. The abstract focuses on encoding and decoding throughput rather than any model pretraining or transferable representation learning.","evidence_snippet":"\"two-bit coding\" and \"encoding and decoding efficiency\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001665","title":"Memo-Patho: Bridging Local-Global Transmembrane Protein Contexts with Contrastive Pretraining for Alignment-Free Pathogenicity Prediction","abstract":"Abstract\n                Understanding the pathogenic impact of protein mutations remains a fundamental challenge in genomic medicine, particularly for transmembrane proteins (TMPs), a functionally critical yet structurally under-annotated class that includes numerous drug targets. Current variant effect predictors often fall short in this domain due to computational burdens, reliance on multiple sequence alignments (MSAs), and limited ability to generalize to TMP-specific constraints. Here we present Memo-Patho, an alignment-free deep learning framework specifically optimized for TMP mutation pathogenicity prediction. Memo-Patho integrates global and local representations from protein language models (PLMs) and predicted structural features. Furthermore, it introduces a contrastive pre-training strategy that learns discriminative features by comparing benign and pathogenic mutations within the same protein backbone. This design enables the model to capture functional disruptions in challenging contexts without structural or evolutionary alignments. Memo-Patho outperforms state-of-the-art predictors on two types of TMP benchmark datasets (accuracy up to 0.93) and unseen-protein generalization tasks, showing strong agreement with evolutionary conservation profiles. Its predictions are further validated on a manually curated KCNQ1 dataset of 55 ion channel variants, achieving an accuracy of 0.84 and outperforming existing tools. The model also supports high-throughput scanning, efficiently analyzing over 15000 variants in under 80 minutes. Our results establish Memo-Patho as a fast, generalizable, and biologically grounded approach for variant effect prediction in TMPs. Its alignment-free design, contrastive learning paradigm, and clinical robustness mark a step forward towards scalable interpretation of TMP variation for both basic research and precision medicine applications.","doi":"10.1101/2025.05.18.654712","year":"2025","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001665","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Uses protein language model representations as features for TMP pathogenicity prediction rather than presenting a substantive text-bio model. The abstract describes a downstream prediction framework, not a text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"integrates global and local representations from protein language models (PLMs)"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001665","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a contrastive pretraining framework for TMP pathogenicity prediction, not a generative architecture. It uses protein language model features but does not show foundation-model-like broad pretraining or broad applicability.","evidence_snippet":"contrastive pre-training strategy ... for TMP mutation pathogenicity prediction"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_001666","title":"Abstract 6339: Deep plasma proteomics at scale enabling proteogenomic analyses in a lung cancer (NSCLC) study","abstract":"Our ~20,000 genes encode over one million protein variants, given alternative splice forms, allelic variation, and protein modification. Though large-scale genomics studies have expanded our understanding of biology, similarly, scaled deep and untargeted proteomics studies of biofluids have remained impractical due to complex workflows. To address this need, we have previously described Proteograph࣪, a novel platform that leverages the protein-corona interactions of nanoparticles for deep and untargeted proteomic sampling at scale.\n Using Proteograph࣪ in a non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) cohort, we previously conducted a deep interrogation of plasma from 141 subjects: 61 early-stage NSCLC subjects and 80 non-cancer controls. We identified 2,499 plasma proteins, with 1,992 present in ≥ 25% of the samples. Leveraging this data, we created a biomarker classifier distinguishing NSCLC from controls with area under the receiver operating characteristic curve of 0.911. In this study, we now re-analyze the data with the more sensitive DIA-NN software to enhance protein depth while preserving the accuracy of the classifier. In addition, to show the added value of our platform in combination with genomic data, we integrate previously sequenced exome data with our proteomic data to build a multi-modal proteogenomic deep learning classifier. Our results outline proteogenomic workflows for robust biomarker discovery and cohort subtyping.\n The Proteograph࣪ platform interrogates the plasma proteome at previously impractical combinations of scale, depth and coverage, and enables the development of improved classification models and the study of proteogenomics.\n 1Blume et al. Nat. Comm. (2020)\n Citation Format: Mahdi Zamanighomi, Harendra Guturu, Jian Wang, Amir Alavi, Tristan Brown, Daniel Hornburg, Moaraj Hasan, Shadi Ferdosi, Khatereh Motamedchaboki, Margaret Donovan, Theodore Platt, Ryan Benz, Asim Siddiqui, Serafim Batzoglou. Deep plasma proteomics at scale enabling proteogenomic analyses in a lung cancer (NSCLC) study [abstract]. In: Proceedings of the American Association for Cancer Research Annual Meeting 2022; 2022 Apr 8-13. Philadelphia (PA): AACR; Cancer Res 2022;82(12_Suppl):Abstract nr 6339.","doi":"10.1158/1538-7445.am2022-6339","year":"2022","venue":"Cancer Research","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001666","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a proteomics/proteogenomic classifier built from plasma proteins and exome data. No text or language component is described in the abstract.","evidence_snippet":"build a multi-modal proteogenomic deep learning classifier"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_001666","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a proteomics/proteogenomic classifier and software re-analysis, not a generative model paper. The abstract focuses on biomarker classification from plasma proteins and exome data.","evidence_snippet":"build a multi-modal proteogenomic deep learning classifier"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0172.stderr.log:490:./data/exports/springernature_2026-02-06.json:26813:    "title": "Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT",
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0172.stderr.log:491:./data/exports/springernature_2026-02-06.json:26837:    "abstract": "Single-cell large language models (scLLMs) capture essential biological insights from vast single-cell atlases but struggle in out-of-context applications, where zero-shot predictions can be unreliable. To address this, here we introduce a single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework that integrates learnable, low-dimensional adapters into scLLMs. By freezing the backbone model and updating only the adapter parameters, scPEFT efficiently adapts to specific tasks using limited custom data. This approach mitigates catastrophic forgetting, reduces parameter tuning by over 96% and decreases GPU memory usage by more than half, thus substantially enhancing the accessibility of scLLMs for resource-constrained researchers. When validated across diverse datasets, scPEFT outperformed zero-shot models and traditional fine-tuning in disease-specific, cross-species and undercharacterized cell population tasks. Its attention-mechanism analysis identified COVID-related genes associated with specific cell states and uncovered unique blood cell subpopulations, demonstrating the capacity of scPEFT for condition-specific interpretations. These findings position scPEFT as an efficient solution for enhancing the utility of scLLMs in general single-cell analyses. He et al. present a parameter-efficient fine-tuning method for single-cell language models that improves performance on unseen diseases, treatments and cell types.",
./data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_09/full_2026-07-06__rec_003141/full_2026_07_06_rec_003141_docling_json_20260710_023516/docling_graph/provenance.json:5566:      "text": "Appendix\nGene Analysis Experiment\nC.2. Model Architectures for Single-Cell Analysis\nTraditional single-cell large language models (LLMs) are predominantly based on transformer architectures (Boiarsky et al., 2023; White & Harris, 2024). While transformers have demonstrated exceptional performance in various single-cell transcriptomics tasks, their high computational and memory requirements make training large models time-intensive and resource-heavy. These limitations become particularly evident when processing the ultra-long sequences typical of single-cell RNA-seq data. To alleviate these challenges, some transformer-based models have incorporated optimizations to improve efficiency. For example, scBERT integrates Performer (Choromanski et al., 2020) to approximate attention calculations, while scGPT employs Flash Attention (Dao et al., 2022) to accelerate training by reducing memory overhead. Despite these advancements, the overall time required to train such models remains substantial, especially for datasets with millions of cells and thousands of genes. To address the intrinsic limitations of transformers, Bidirectional Mamba (Bi-Mamba) has been proposed as a novel alternative architecture. Bi-Mamba leverages state-space models (SSMs) to process ultra-long sequences with linear computational complexity in both time and memory. This represents a significant departure from traditional transformer-based methods, offering a scalable and efficient solution for the high-dimensional, high-throughput nature of single-cell transcriptomics data.",
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0110.stderr.log:1476:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/adjudicator.jsonl:68:{"record_id": "rec_000249", "paper_type": "application_wrapper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "generative_model_present": "yes", "foundation_model_evidence": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "decision_rationale": "Although the abstract uses scLLMs, the contribution is a parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework around an existing backbone, not a new foundation model. The reviewer conflict is resolved conservatively as an application wrapper.", "evidence_snippet": "\"single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework\" and \"freezing the backbone model\""}
./data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_09/full_2026-07-06__rec_003141/full_2026_07_06_rec_003141_docling_json_20260710_023516/docling/chunks.json:5553:    "text": "Appendix\nGene Analysis Experiment\nC.2. Model Architectures for Single-Cell Analysis\nTraditional single-cell large language models (LLMs) are predominantly based on transformer architectures (Boiarsky et al., 2023; White & Harris, 2024). While transformers have demonstrated exceptional performance in various single-cell transcriptomics tasks, their high computational and memory requirements make training large models time-intensive and resource-heavy. These limitations become particularly evident when processing the ultra-long sequences typical of single-cell RNA-seq data. To alleviate these challenges, some transformer-based models have incorporated optimizations to improve efficiency. For example, scBERT integrates Performer (Choromanski et al., 2020) to approximate attention calculations, while scGPT employs Flash Attention (Dao et al., 2022) to accelerate training by reducing memory overhead. Despite these advancements, the overall time required to train such models remains substantial, especially for datasets with millions of cells and thousands of genes. To address the intrinsic limitations of transformers, Bidirectional Mamba (Bi-Mamba) has been proposed as a novel alternative architecture. Bi-Mamba leverages state-space models (SSMs) to process ultra-long sequences with linear computational complexity in both time and memory. This represents a significant departure from traditional transformer-based methods, offering a scalable and efficient solution for the high-dimensional, high-throughput nature of single-cell transcriptomics data.",
./data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_09/full_2026-07-06__rec_003141/full_2026_07_06_rec_003141_docling_json_20260710_023516/docling/document.json:12407:      "orig": "Traditional single-cell large language models (LLMs) are predominantly based on transformer architectures (Boiarsky et al., 2023; White & Harris, 2024). While transformers have demonstrated exceptional performance in various single-cell transcriptomics tasks, their high computational and memory requirements make training large models time-intensive and resource-heavy. These limitations become particularly evident when processing the ultra-long sequences typical of single-cell RNA-seq data. To alleviate these challenges, some transformer-based models have incorporated optimizations to improve efficiency. For example, scBERT integrates Performer (Choromanski et al., 2020) to approximate attention calculations, while scGPT employs Flash Attention (Dao et al., 2022) to accelerate training by reducing memory overhead. Despite these advancements, the overall time required to train such models remains substantial, especially for datasets with millions of cells and thousands of genes. To address the intrinsic limitations of transformers, Bidirectional Mamba (Bi-Mamba) has been proposed as a novel alternative architecture. Bi-Mamba leverages state-space models (SSMs) to process ultra-long sequences with linear computational complexity in both time and memory. This represents a significant departure from traditional transformer-based methods, offering a scalable and efficient solution for the high-dimensional, high-throughput nature of single-cell transcriptomics data.",
./data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_09/full_2026-07-06__rec_003141/full_2026_07_06_rec_003141_docling_json_20260710_023516/docling/document.json:12408:      "text": "Traditional single-cell large language models (LLMs) are predominantly based on transformer architectures (Boiarsky et al., 2023; White & Harris, 2024). While transformers have demonstrated exceptional performance in various single-cell transcriptomics tasks, their high computational and memory requirements make training large models time-intensive and resource-heavy. These limitations become particularly evident when processing the ultra-long sequences typical of single-cell RNA-seq data. To alleviate these challenges, some transformer-based models have incorporated optimizations to improve efficiency. For example, scBERT integrates Performer (Choromanski et al., 2020) to approximate attention calculations, while scGPT employs Flash Attention (Dao et al., 2022) to accelerate training by reducing memory overhead. Despite these advancements, the overall time required to train such models remains substantial, especially for datasets with millions of cells and thousands of genes. To address the intrinsic limitations of transformers, Bidirectional Mamba (Bi-Mamba) has been proposed as a novel alternative architecture. Bi-Mamba leverages state-space models (SSMs) to process ultra-long sequences with linear computational complexity in both time and memory. This represents a significant departure from traditional transformer-based methods, offering a scalable and efficient solution for the high-dimensional, high-throughput nature of single-cell transcriptomics data."
./data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_09/full_2026-07-06__rec_003141/full_2026_07_06_rec_003141_docling_json_20260710_023516/docling/document.dclg:3401:<![CDATA[Traditional single-cell large language models (LLMs) are predominantly based on transformer architectures (Boiarsky et al., 2023; White & Harris, 2024). While transformers have demonstrated exceptional performance in various single-cell transcriptomics tasks, their high computational and memory requirements make training large models time-intensive and resource-heavy. These limitations become particularly evident when processing the ultra-long sequences typical of single-cell RNA-seq data. To alleviate these challenges, some transformer-based models have incorporated optimizations to improve efficiency. For example, scBERT integrates Performer (Choromanski et al., 2020) to approximate attention calculations, while scGPT employs Flash Attention (Dao et al., 2022) to accelerate training by reducing memory overhead. Despite these advancements, the overall time required to train such models remains substantial, especially for datasets with millions of cells and thousands of genes. To address the intrinsic limitations of transformers, Bidirectional Mamba (Bi-Mamba) has been proposed as a novel alternative architecture. Bi-Mamba leverages state-space models (SSMs) to process ultra-long sequences with linear computational complexity in both time and memory. This represents a significant departure from traditional transformer-based methods, offering a scalable and efficient solution for the high-dimensional, high-throughput nature of single-cell transcriptomics data.]]>  </text>
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0482.stderr.log:62:[{"record_id":"rec_003849","title":"scUnify: A Unified Framework for Zero-shot Inference of Single-Cell Foundation Models","abstract":"Abstract\n                \n                  Foundation models (FMs) pre-trained on large-scale single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data provide powerful cell embeddings, but their practical usability and systematic comparison are limited by model-specific environments, preprocessing pipelines, and execution procedures. To address these challenges, we introduce scUnify, a unified zero-shot inference framework for single-cell foundation models. scUnify accepts a standard AnnData object and automatically manages environment isolation, preprocessing, and tokenization through a registry-based modular design. It employs a hierarchical distributed inference strategy that combines Ray-based task scheduling with multi-GPU data-parallel execution via HuggingFace Accelerate, enabling scalable inference on datasets containing up to one million cells. In addition, built-in integration of scIB and scGraph metrics enables standardized cross-model embedding evaluation within a single workflow. Benchmarking results demonstrate substantial reductions in inference time compared with the original model implementations, while preserving embedding quality and achieving near-linear multi-GPU scaling. scUnify is implemented in Python and is publicly available at\n                  https://github.com/DHKim327/scUnify\n                  .","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003850","title":"Evaluating the learnability of single-cell large language models on multiple tasks","abstract":"Abstract\n                The rise of single-cell foundation models (scFMs) has sparked interest in their potential to unify diverse biological tasks. However, their practical utility and the validity of scaling laws—the assumption that performance improves with model and data size—remain under-examined. Here, we systematically evaluate two representative scFMs, Geneformer and scGPT, across perturbation prediction and cell type annotation tasks. Our findings suggest that the benefits of large-scale pretraining are strongly task-dependent, conferring substantial advantages in cell type annotation but limited gains in perturbation prediction. Furthermore, our results indicate that increasing model size does not guarantee improved performance and can even be detrimental, challenging the ``bigger is better'' paradigm. By comparing model performance on real versus synthetic data with different levels of complexity, our analysis suggests that for perturbation prediction, the tested scFMs capture little more than simple summary statistics and may struggle to learn complex biological interactions. These results highlight the need to move beyond scaling and toward developing models that integrate deeper biological knowledge. We suggest that a renewed focus on task-specific architectures and biologically-informed priors may be critical for unlocking the true potential of foundation models in single-cell biology.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003851","title":"Cell phenotypes in the biomedical literature: a systematic analysis and text mining corpus","abstract":"Abstract\n                The variety of cell phenotypes identified by single-cell technologies is rapidly expanding, yet this knowledge is dispersed across the scientific literature and incompletely represented in structured resources. We present the CellLink corpus, a manually annotated collection of over 22,000 mentions of human and mouse cell populations in recent journal articles, distinguishing specific cell phenotypes, heterogeneous cell populations, and vague cell populations, and linking to Cell Ontology (CL) terms as either exact or related matches, covering nearly half of the terms in the current CL. A systematic analysis reveals lineage-specific patterns in how authors utilize anatomical context, molecular signatures, functional roles, developmental stage, and other attributes in cell naming. We show that fine-tuning transformer-based models on CellLink yields strong performance for named entity recognition, while embedding-based approaches support zero-shot entity linking and distinguishing exact from related matches. We further demonstrate the utility of CellLink to expand and refine the chondrocyte branch of CL.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003852","title":"A Generative Foundation Model for Multimodal Histopathology","abstract":"in natural language prompts. We benchmarked MUPAD against three baseline models:   We employed Histoplus 36 to perform downstream cell-type composition analysis by classifying","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"arXiv preprint arXiv …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003853","title":"From Modality Translation to Regulatory Reasoning: Interpretable AI for Single-Cell Multi-Omic Systems","abstract":"Building on this foundation, we contribute to population- 42 ix 3.4 Determining cell type–specific  eQTLs from single-cell  commonly utilized in natural language processing models as well","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003854","title":"Learning to Cluster Rare Cell Types: Implicit Semantic Data Augmentation for Spatial Multi-modal Omics Analysis","abstract":"Spatial multi-modal omics technologies have transformed biological research by enabling the simultaneous profiling of gene expression, protein abundance, and chromatin accessibility within their native spatial contexts. Despite these advances, accurately clustering rare cell types remains a major challenge due to data sparsity, high dimensionality, and limited annotated samples. While Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) have shown potential in modeling spatial omics data, their effectiveness is often constrained by the use of fixed K-nearest neighbor (KNN) graph structures, which fail to capture latent semantic relationships masked by sequencing noise. To overcome these limitations, we propose CRCT (Clustering Rare Cell Types): a novel framework that combines Implicit Semantic Data Augmentation (ISDA) with adaptive graph learning for spatial multi-modal omics analysis. Unlike traditional augmentation strategies that generate explicit synthetic samples, CRCT operates in the deep feature space by dynamically estimating intra-class covariance matrices and implicitly perturbing features along semantically meaningful directions. This enables effective augmentation for rare cell populations while preserving biological fidelity. Extensive experiments across four real-world datasets (HLN, MB, Stereo‑CITE‑seq, and SPOTS) and one synthetic benchmark demonstrate the state-of-the-art performance of CRCT, achieving improvements of up to +1.7 NMI and +7.8 ARI over strong baseline methods.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Proceedings of the AAAI …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003855","title":"Advancing single-cell omics and cell-based therapeutics with quantum computing","abstract":"of single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data, cell-type  have been proposed for  performing natural language  models to complex generative and AI foundation models 125","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Reviews Molecular …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003856","title":"Modelling drug-induced cellular perturbation responses with a biologically informed dual-branch transformer","abstract":"architectures in single-cell large language models, we adopt  similar to word tokens in natural  language processing 51 , in  task that aims to classify the cell type based on the \\(< \\mathrm{","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Machine …","sources":["google_scholar"]}]
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0482.stderr.log:102:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_003849","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a unified zero-shot inference framework for existing single-cell foundation models, not a new generative model. The abstract focuses on preprocessing, tokenization, and distributed inference.","evidence_snippet":"\"introduce scUnify, a unified zero-shot inference framework for single-cell foundation models\" and \"automatically manages environment isolation, preprocessing, and tokenization\""},{"record_id":"rec_003850","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper evaluates existing single-cell large language models rather than introducing a new model. It clearly discusses large-scale pretraining and task transfer, but the abstract does not explicitly establish a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically evaluate two representative scFMs, Geneformer and scGPT\" and \"benefits of large-scale pretraining\""},{"record_id":"rec_003851","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a manually annotated corpus and text-mining resource, not a generative model paper. The abstract describes NER, entity linking, and ontology expansion.","evidence_snippet":"\"manually annotated collection\" and \"embedding-based approaches support zero-shot entity linking\""},{"record_id":"rec_003852","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title explicitly presents a generative foundation model, and the abstract mentions natural language prompts and benchmarking of the model. That supports both generative status and foundation-model framing.","evidence_snippet":"\"A Generative Foundation Model for Multimodal Histopathology\" and \"natural language prompts\""},{"record_id":"rec_003853","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The provided text is too fragmentary to identify the work type or determine whether it describes a generative foundation model. It contains isolated technical phrases but no clear model description.","evidence_snippet":"fragmentary text such as \"From Modality Translation to Regulatory Reasoning\" and \"commonly utilized in natural language processing models\""},{"record_id":"rec_003854","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes clustering rare cell types with graph learning and implicit data augmentation, which is predictive rather than generative. No foundation-model-like pretraining or broad transfer is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"novel framework that combines Implicit Semantic Data Augmentation (ISDA) with adaptive graph learning\" and \"clustering rare cell types\""},{"record_id":"rec_003855","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The title indicates a review-style article on single-cell omics and therapeutics rather than a primary model paper. The snippet is too incomplete to establish a specific generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"Advancing single-cell omics and cell-based therapeutics with quantum computing\""},{"record_id":"rec_003856","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a biologically informed transformer for modeling perturbation responses and cell-type classification. That is a predictive setup, not an explicitly generative architecture or foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"dual-branch transformer\" and \"task that aims to classify the cell type\""}]}
./data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_09/full_2026-07-06__rec_003141/full_2026_07_06_rec_003141_docling_json_20260710_023516/docling/document.md:537:Traditional single-cell large language models (LLMs) are predominantly based on transformer architectures (Boiarsky et al., 2023; White &amp; Harris, 2024). While transformers have demonstrated exceptional performance in various single-cell transcriptomics tasks, their high computational and memory requirements make training large models time-intensive and resource-heavy. These limitations become particularly evident when processing the ultra-long sequences typical of single-cell RNA-seq data. To alleviate these challenges, some transformer-based models have incorporated optimizations to improve efficiency. For example, scBERT integrates Performer (Choromanski et al., 2020) to approximate attention calculations, while scGPT employs Flash Attention (Dao et al., 2022) to accelerate training by reducing memory overhead. Despite these advancements, the overall time required to train such models remains substantial, especially for datasets with millions of cells and thousands of genes. To address the intrinsic limitations of transformers, Bidirectional Mamba (Bi-Mamba) has been proposed as a novel alternative architecture. Bi-Mamba leverages state-space models (SSMs) to process ultra-long sequences with linear computational complexity in both time and memory. This represents a significant departure from traditional transformer-based methods, offering a scalable and efficient solution for the high-dimensional, high-throughput nature of single-cell transcriptomics data.
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0234.stderr.log:1197:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/adjudicator.jsonl:68:{"record_id": "rec_000249", "paper_type": "application_wrapper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "generative_model_present": "yes", "foundation_model_evidence": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "decision_rationale": "Although the abstract uses scLLMs, the contribution is a parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework around an existing backbone, not a new foundation model. The reviewer conflict is resolved conservatively as an application wrapper.", "evidence_snippet": "\"single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework\" and \"freezing the backbone model\""}
