./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0173.prompt.txt:47:[{"record_id":"rec_001377","title":"tRNAs as a Driving Force of Genome Evolution in Yeast","abstract":"Transfer RNAs (tRNAs) are widely known for their roles in the decoding of the linear mRNA information into amino acid sequences of proteins. They are also multifunctional platforms in the translation process and have other roles beyond translation, including sensing amino acid abundance, interacting with the general stress response machinery, and modulating cellular adaptation, survival, and death. In this mini-review, we focus on the emerging role of tRNA genes in the organization and modification of the genomic architecture of yeast and the role of tRNA misexpression and decoding infidelity in genome stability, evolution, and adaption. We discuss published work showing how quickly tRNA genes can mutate to meet novel translational demands, how tRNAs speed up genome evolution, and how tRNA genes can be sites of genomic instability. We highlight recent works showing that loss of tRNA decoding fidelity and small alterations in tRNA expression have unexpected and profound impacts on genome stability. By dissecting these recent evidence, we hope to lay the groundwork that prompts future investigations on the mechanistic interplay between tRNAs and genome modification that likely triggers genome evolution.","doi":"10.3389/fmicb.2021.634004","year":"2021","venue":"Frontiers in Microbiology","sources":["semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_001378","title":"A deep generative model integrating single-cell time-frequency characteristics transformed from electrophysiological data with transcriptomic features","abstract":"Patch-seq yields multi-modal data (e.g., transcriptomic and electrophysiological data) from single cells. However, currently used analytical methods are based on a few global electrophysiological features predefined from chronological potential changes and overlook the importance of time-frequency domain. In this study, we present LincSpectr, a deep neural network model that integrates transcriptomic and electrophysiological features with the latent variables of various variational autoencoders. This model combines the two modalities according to the connection between the latent variables of different modalities calculated by attention-like mechanisms and achieves cross-modal predictions and an inverse analysis. We discovered that the predicted electrophysiological features changed continuously along with their transcriptional profiles and that the neighborhood relationships between the latent states of the transcriptional profiles were consistent with those of the electrophysiological features. Inverse analysis of our model enabled the extraction of gene sets affecting specific time-frequency domains; some genes were likely to be involved in neural activity. Our approach represents a potential avenue to facilitate the discovery of molecular mechanisms underlying time-frequency activities in various cell types, enhancing our understanding of their roles in neural function.","doi":"10.1101/2024.03.29.587341","year":"2024","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["biorxiv_medrxiv","semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_001379","title":"DeepMPF: deep learning framework for predicting drug–target interactions based on multi-modal representation with meta-path semantic analysis","abstract":"Background Drug-target interaction (DTI) prediction has become a crucial prerequisite in drug design and drug discovery. However, the traditional biological experiment is time-consuming and expensive, as there are abundant complex interactions present in the large size of genomic and chemical spaces. For alleviating this phenomenon, plenty of computational methods are conducted to effectively complement biological experiments and narrow the search spaces into a preferred candidate domain. Whereas, most of the previous approaches cannot fully consider association behavior semantic information based on several schemas to represent complex the structure of heterogeneous biological networks. Additionally, the prediction of DTI based on single modalities cannot satisfy the demand for prediction accuracy. Methods We propose a multi-modal representation framework of ‘DeepMPF’ based on meta-path semantic analysis, which effectively utilizes heterogeneous information to predict DTI. Specifically, we first construct protein–drug-disease heterogeneous networks composed of three entities. Then the feature information is obtained under three views, containing sequence modality, heterogeneous structure modality and similarity modality. We proposed six representative schemas of meta-path to preserve the high-order nonlinear structure and catch hidden structural information of the heterogeneous network. Finally, DeepMPF generates highly representative comprehensive feature descriptors and calculates the probability of interaction through joint learning. Results To evaluate the predictive performance of DeepMPF, comparison experiments are conducted on four gold datasets. Our method can obtain competitive performance in all datasets. We also explore the influence of the different feature embedding dimensions, learning strategies and classification methods. Meaningfully, the drug repositioning experiments on COVID-19 and HIV demonstrate DeepMPF can be applied to solve problems in reality and help drug discovery. The further analysis of molecular docking experiments enhances the credibility of the drug candidates predicted by DeepMPF. Conclusions All the results demonstrate the effectively predictive capability of DeepMPF for drug-target interactions. It can be utilized as a useful tool to prescreen the most potential drug candidates for the protein. The web server of the DeepMPF predictor is freely available at http://120.77.11.78/DeepMPF/ , which can help relevant researchers to further study.","doi":"10.1186/s12967-023-03876-3","year":"2023","venue":"Journal of Translational Medicine","sources":["semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_001380","title":"Abstract 2085: Visium HD combined with deep-learning-based cell segmentation on H&E images yield accurate cell annotation at single-cell resolution","abstract":"Bulk and single-cell next-generation sequencing (NGS) have been instrumental tools for characterizing gene expression profiles of tumor samples. However, the lack of spatial and cellular context limits their utility in investigating tissue architecture and cellular interactions in the tumor microenvironment (TME). NGS-based Spatial Transcriptomics (ST) technologies have gained increasing attention for their ability to provide spatial context of gene expression, but they have been constrained by low resolutions until the recent launch of 10X Genomics Visium HD platform. This platform achieves whole-transcriptome profiling at 2 μm resolution. However, there are complications in downstream biological interpretations with the default binning of Visium HD at 8 μm resolution.\n \n \n \n Primary tumor samples were collected from 2 patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). The 10X Genomics Visium HD platform was used to generate high-resolution ST data. To study spatial expression at the single-cell level, we trained a cell-segmentation neural network and applied it to H&E images, generating cell segmentation masks. These masks were used to summarize raw Visium HD data at 2 μm resolution into single-cell-level gene counts. Clustering was performed on the generated single-cell level data and a large language model-based (LLM-based) cell type annotator was used to infer the cell types of each cluster.\n \n \n \n The LLM-annotated cell clusters from Visium HD were highly consistent with pathologist’s annotations based on H&E images. Specifically, in the immune-active sample, 93.1% of cells in annotated lymphocyte regions were classified as lymphocytes based on cell-level ST counts, and 88.8% of cells identified in annotated tumor regions were classified as cancer cells. Cell-level ST clustering also identified a benign epithelium cell cluster that is confirmed by pathologist’s annotations. Similarly, in the immune-inactive sample, 72.8% of cells in annotated tumor regions and 95.8% of cells in annotated stroma regions were classified as cancer cells and stromal cells respectively.\n \n \n \n Our findings showcase the feasibility and advantages of analyzing Visium HD data at single-cell resolution using deep-learning-based segmentation models applied to H&E images, empowering clinical biomarker discovery and new mechanistic insights.\n \n \n \n Tianyou Luo, Mario G. Rosasco, Christine M. Hoeman, Sonal Khare, Daniel J. Gorski, Tim A. Rand, Richard A. Klinghoffer, Justin Guinney, Chi-Sing Ho. Visium HD combined with deep-learning-based cell segmentation on H&E images yield accurate cell annotation at single-cell resolution [abstract]. In: Proceedings of the American Association for Cancer Research Annual Meeting 2025; Part 1 (Regular Abstracts); 2025 Apr 25-30; Chicago, IL. Philadelphia (PA): AACR; Cancer Res 2025;85(8_Suppl_1):Abstract nr 2085.","doi":"10.1158/1538-7445.am2025-2085","year":"2025","venue":"Cancer Research","sources":["semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_001381","title":"Language-Enhanced Representation Learning for Single-Cell Transcriptomics","abstract":"Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) offers detailed insights into cellular heterogeneity. Recent advancements leverage single-cell large language models (scLLMs) for effective representation learning. These models focus exclusively on transcriptomic data, neglecting complementary biological knowledge from textual descriptions. To overcome this limitation, we propose scMMGPT, a novel multimodal framework designed for language-enhanced representation learning in single-cell transcriptomics. Unlike existing methods, scMMGPT employs robust cell representation extraction, preserving quantitative gene expression data, and introduces an innovative two-stage pre-training strategy combining discriminative precision with generative flexibility. Extensive experiments demonstrate that scMMGPT significantly outperforms unimodal and multimodal baselines across key downstream tasks, including cell annotation and clustering, and exhibits superior generalization in out-of-distribution scenarios.","doi":"","year":"2025","venue":"","sources":["arxiv","google_scholar","semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_001382","title":"A Multimodal Data Analytics Framework for Early Cancer Detection Using Genomic, Radiomic, and Clinical Big Data Fusion","abstract":"Early detection of cancer has a great impact on increasing patient survival, on reducing treatment intensity and on lowering healthcare costs in the long term. Despite advancements in genomics, imaging, biomarkers and EHR analytics, it's difficult for current tools, due to the various sources of data. This makes it difficult to detect cancers when patients have few, if any, symptoms. In order to address this issue, we propose a Multimodal Data Analytics Framework (MDAF). It integrates genomic sequences, imaging features extracted using radiomics and large clinical data sets. It involves AI and deep-learning pipelines that handle various types of data. The framework has performed data ingestion, automatic feature extraction, hierarchical harmonization, transformer-based fusion, predictive modeling and explainable AI (XAI). It also operates with the privacy-preserving technique of federated learning. Previous studies indicate that data combination from multiple types yields better results than a single-type combination. The accuracy, sensitivity, and specificity can on average be improved by 10-35% using multimodal fusion in different cancers. MDAF is based on the principles of precision oncology, and applicable in real hospitals with their information systems. It is conducive to personalized and evidence-based decisions for clinicians. Future work involves construction of the digital twin simulations for oncology, national federated research registries, and multimodal biomedical knowledge graphs.","doi":"10.32996/jcsts.2023.5.3.13","year":"2023","venue":"Journal of Computer Science and Technology Studies","sources":["semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_001383","title":"Abstract 991: PARADIGM: an embeddings-based multimodal learning framework with foundation models and graph neural networks","abstract":"Cancer research faces significant challenges in integrating heterogeneous data across varying spatial and temporal scales, limiting the ability to gain a comprehensive understanding of the disease. PARADIGM(Pan-Cancer Embeddings Representation using Advanced Multimodal Learning with Graph-based Modeling) addresses this challenge by providing a framework leveraging foundation models (FMs) and Graph Neural Networks (GNN). PARADIGM framework generates embeddings from multi-resolution datasets using modality-specific FMs, aggregates sample embeddings, fuses them into a unified graph representation, and uses GNNs for survival prediction.\n \n \n \n We applied PARADIGM for survival analysis of five squamous cell carcinomas; head and neck (527), lung (496), bladder (408), cervical (294), and esophageal (184) cancers, and five adenocarcinomas; colon (393), lung (549), prostate (490), rectum (157), and stomach (406) cancers. Additionally, we validated using lung squamous cell data from Moffitt Cancer Center (103 patients). Multimodal data for training and evaluation included EHR data (e.g., age, gender, and smoking status), whole slide images (WSIs), pathology reports, and molecular data (e.g., gene expression, miRNA, and protein expression). Model performance was assessed using concordance index (C-index) for survival prediction, with 7-fold cross-validation ensuring robustness.\n \n \n \n PARADIGM demonstrated significant improvements in survival prediction compared to unimodal and multimodal approaches such as multilayer perceptron, Transformers, and XGBoost (Table 1). Our findings indicate that integrating diverse data modalities into a cohesive representation yields more accurate and insightful predictions. This approach highlights the potential of embeddings-based multimodal learning to foster improved outcome predictions and provide actionable insights for personalized treatment strategies.\n \n \n \n Asim Waqas,  Aakash Tripathi,  Mia Naeini,  Paul A. Stewart,  Matthew B. Schabath,  Ghulam Rasool. PARADIGM: an embeddings-based multimodal learning framework with foundation models and graph neural networks [abstract]. In: Proceedings of the American Association for Cancer Research Annual Meeting 2025; Part 1 (Regular Abstracts); 2025 Apr 25-30; Chicago, IL. Philadelphia (PA): AACR; Cancer Res 2025;85(8_Suppl_1):Abstract nr 991.","doi":"10.1158/1538-7445.am2025-991","year":"2025","venue":"Cancer Research","sources":["semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_001384","title":"HEST-1k: A Dataset for Spatial Transcriptomics and Histology Image Analysis","abstract":"Spatial transcriptomics enables interrogating the molecular composition of tissue with ever-increasing resolution and sensitivity. However, costs, rapidly evolving technology, and lack of standards have constrained computational methods in ST to narrow tasks and small cohorts. In addition, the underlying tissue morphology, as reflected by H&E-stained whole slide images (WSIs), encodes rich information often overlooked in ST studies. Here, we introduce HEST-1k, a collection of 1,229 spatial transcriptomic profiles, each linked to a WSI and extensive metadata. HEST-1k was assembled from 153 public and internal cohorts encompassing 26 organs, two species (Homo Sapiens and Mus Musculus), and 367 cancer samples from 25 cancer types. HEST-1k processing enabled the identification of 2.1 million expression--morphology pairs and over 76 million nuclei. To support its development, we additionally introduce the HEST-Library, a Python package designed to perform a range of actions with HEST samples. We test HEST-1k and Library on three use cases: (1) benchmarking foundation models for pathology (HEST-Benchmark), (2) biomarker exploration, and (3) multimodal representation learning. HEST-1k, HEST-Library, and HEST-Benchmark can be freely accessed at https://github.com/mahmoodlab/hest.","doi":"10.48550/arXiv.2406.16192","year":"2024","venue":"Neural Information Processing Systems","sources":["semantic_scholar"]}]
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0170.stdout.log:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_001353","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a prognostic test that extracts features from an existing vision transformer foundation model and combines them with clinical data. It does not describe a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"“utilized a vision transformer pan-cancer foundation model trained with self-supervised learning to extract features”"},{"record_id":"rec_001354","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a systematic review of AI studies, not a primary model paper. The abstract summarizes literature on diagnosis performance rather than proposing a generative model.","evidence_snippet":"“systematically evaluate the diagnostic performance of artificial intelligence” and “A total of 44 studies met the inclusion criteria”"},{"record_id":"rec_001355","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The method uses CNN, self-attention, LSTM, and GNN for prediction and classification. No generative mechanism or foundation-model-style pretraining is described.","evidence_snippet":"“gene expression prediction” and “regulatory element classification tasks”"},{"record_id":"rec_001356","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"SeqMate is a pipeline that uses a large language model to automate RNA-seq analysis and generate written reports. The abstract does not give enough detail about the underlying model’s pretraining or foundation-model characteristics.","evidence_snippet":"“utilizing the power of a large language model (LLM)” and “producing written reports”"},{"record_id":"rec_001357","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract is about whole-genome sequencing and bioinformatics annotation of a bacterial isolate. It does not describe any generative model or foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“Whole genome sequencing emerged as the methodological linchpin” and “bioinformatics analysis”"},{"record_id":"rec_001358","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework built on existing single-cell large language models. The abstract explicitly references broad zero-shot use and adapting a frozen backbone rather than introducing a new base model.","evidence_snippet":"“single-cell large language models (scLLMs)” and “freezing the backbone model and updating only the adapter parameters”"},{"record_id":"rec_001359","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"NicheTrans is presented as a Transformer-based cross-omics translation framework, but the abstract does not explicitly state a decoder, autoregressive, or seq2seq generative mechanism. It also does not provide evidence of broad pretraining or foundation-model-like scope.","evidence_snippet":"“first, spatially-aware cross-omics translation method” and “Transformer-based multi-modal deep learning framework”"},{"record_id":"rec_001360","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a review of AI applications in pulmonary medicine, not a primary model paper. The abstract surveys computer vision, predictive modeling, and COVID-19 applications rather than describing a generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“The goal of this review is to provide pulmonary specialists” and “we review some of the literature”"}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0248.stderr.log:203:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0482.stdout.log:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_003849","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"A unified zero-shot inference framework for existing single-cell foundation models, not a new candidate model. The abstract focuses on environment isolation, preprocessing, tokenization, and benchmarking of model embeddings.","evidence_snippet":"“unified zero-shot inference framework for single-cell foundation models” and “manages environment isolation, preprocessing, and tokenization”"},{"record_id":"rec_003850","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an evaluation study of existing single-cell large language models across tasks, rather than a new model paper. It benchmarks Geneformer and scGPT on perturbation prediction and cell type annotation.","evidence_snippet":"“systematically evaluate two representative scFMs, Geneformer and scGPT” across tasks"},{"record_id":"rec_003851","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a manually annotated text corpus and text mining utilities, not a new generative model. It links literature mentions of cell phenotypes to Cell Ontology terms.","evidence_snippet":"“manually annotated collection” and “fine-tuning transformer-based models on CellLink”"},{"record_id":"rec_003852","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title presents a generative foundation model for multimodal histopathology, and the abstract mentions natural language prompts. This supports a substantive bio-plus-text model rather than a wrapper.","evidence_snippet":"“A Generative Foundation Model for Multimodal Histopathology” and “natural language prompts”"},{"record_id":"rec_003853","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title suggests an AI systems paper, but the provided abstract text is too fragmentary to establish whether it is a primary model paper or a review-style piece. The text-bio modeling details are not clear from the snippet.","evidence_snippet":"“From Modality Translation to Regulatory Reasoning: Interpretable AI for Single-Cell Multi-Omic Systems” with only fragmentary abstract text"},{"record_id":"rec_003854","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary spatial multi-modal omics method paper, but the abstract contains no substantive text or language component. It focuses on gene expression, protein abundance, chromatin accessibility, and graph learning.","evidence_snippet":"“Spatial multi-modal omics technologies” and “adaptive graph learning for spatial multi-modal omics analysis”"},{"record_id":"rec_003855","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The venue and framing indicate a review article on a broad topic rather than a primary model paper. The abstract fragment is about advancing single-cell omics and therapeutics with quantum computing.","evidence_snippet":"Title/venue framing: “Nature Reviews Molecular …” and “Advancing single-cell omics and cell-based therapeutics with quantum computing”"},{"record_id":"rec_003856","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title describes a biologically informed dual-branch transformer for drug-induced cellular perturbation responses, which is a primary model paper. The abstract fragment also references single-cell large language model architectures and NLP-style tokens.","evidence_snippet":"“biologically informed dual-branch transformer” and “single-cell large language models” / “word tokens in natural language processing”"}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0248.stderr.log:1630:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0130.stderr.log:283:/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0234.stderr.log:1197:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/adjudicator.jsonl:68:{"record_id": "rec_000249", "paper_type": "application_wrapper", "bio_modality_present": "yes", "text_component_present": "yes", "text_bio_bridge_present": "no", "generative_model_present": "yes", "foundation_model_evidence": "no", "primary_exclusion_code": "application_wrapper", "uncertainty_reason": "none", "decision_rationale": "Although the abstract uses scLLMs, the contribution is a parameter-efficient fine-tuning framework around an existing backbone, not a new foundation model. The reviewer conflict is resolved conservatively as an application wrapper.", "evidence_snippet": "\"single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework\" and \"freezing the backbone model\""}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0248.stderr.log:2015:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0208.response.txt:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_001657","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Abstract frames the work as benchmarking BCR sequencing technologies and explicitly says it generates a resource for the community. It is about immune repertoire sequencing, not a text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“benchmarking experiment” and “generated a resource for the immunology community”"},{"record_id":"rec_001658","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a Comment that discusses the landscape and barriers around single-cell large language models rather than presenting a new model. It mentions biomedical single-cell omics and LLMs, but not a specific primary text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"“In this Comment, we examine the current landscape of scLLMs”"},{"record_id":"rec_001659","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_component_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract reads as a forward-looking commentary on virtual cells, organs, and foundation models rather than a primary model paper. It does not clearly specify a substantive text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"“Navigating the Deep Waters of Medical AI Models” and “Ultimately, the trajectory...”"},{"record_id":"rec_001660","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This presents a new generative self-expression model for single-cell and spatial multimodal data. The abstract describes omics integration and representation learning, but no substantive text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"“we propose a contrastive and generative deep self-expression model” for “single-cell and spatial multimodal data”"},{"record_id":"rec_001661","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The work reuses pre-trained DNA language models and compares embedding-based pipelines versus fine-tuning for downstream genomic prediction. That is an application of existing models, not a new substantive text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“Large pre-trained DNA language models” and “embedding-based pipelines”"},{"record_id":"rec_001662","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"FEDKEA integrates a pretrained protein language model with KNN for enzyme annotation, which is a downstream application pipeline around existing model embeddings. The abstract does not support a new text-bio foundation model with a substantive bridge.","evidence_snippet":"“integrates ESM-2 and distance-weighted KNN” for “enzyme function annotation”"},{"record_id":"rec_001663","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a genomic data compression/encoding paper, not a generative text-bio model paper. The abstract describes direct coding of DNA bases and does not mention any text or language component.","evidence_snippet":"“two-bit coding” for “genomic sequence” compression"},{"record_id":"rec_001664","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says this review synthesizes the technology and applications of droplet-based scRNA-seq. It is a review, not a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"“This review synthesizes the technological evolution” of droplet-based single-cell RNA sequencing"}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0155.stderr.log:2687:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0012.prompt.txt:52:[{"record_id":"rec_000240","title":"Agentic Authoring of Interactive Multiview Visualizations in Genomics","abstract":"Diverse genomics data, scientific questions, and analysis tasks typically demand highly specialized visualizations. Therefore, users often must customize or author new ones tailored to their data. Existing tools are usually either limited in customization or require substantial learning or programming, and even expressive tools assume visualization expertise many users lack. Agentic and large language model (LLM) approaches are increasingly applied to complex scientific tasks, including visualization. Natural-language conversational interfaces offer a promising path to democratizing the authoring of complex visualizations. In the context of genomics, these approaches face additional challenges: genomics visualizations typically integrate heterogeneous data types and are composed of multiple linked interactive views. These challenges motivate more structured LLM-based schemes. We first characterize where vanilla LLM generation succeeds and fails for genomics visualization, identifying eight quality dimensions. We then compare six schemes--direct generation, a fixed pipeline, and four agentic configurations varying in the number of specialist agents and the presence of a reviewer--across 159 cases spanning three levels of query ambiguity and specification complexity. All schemes use the Gosling visualization grammar as structured output. Agentic iteration substantially improves perceived quality over both baselines, while more complex agent architectures yield no additional benefit. We discuss implications for designing agentic systems for domain-specific visualization authoring. All supplemental materials are available at https://osf.io/uqe83.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["arxiv"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000240","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The work studies agentic LLM schemes for authoring genomics visualizations, not a candidate bio-foundational model. The LLM is used as a tooling layer for visualization generation.","evidence_snippet":"Agentic and large language model (LLM) approaches are increasingly applied to complex scientific tasks, including visualization"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000240","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper studies LLM-based generation of visualization grammars, so a generative workflow is present. But it is an agentic authoring/application paper, and the abstract does not establish foundation-model-style pretraining for a candidate text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"“vanilla LLM generation” and “All schemes use the Gosling visualization grammar as structured output”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000249","title":"Harnessing the power of single-cell large language models with parameter-efficient fine-tuning using scPEFT","abstract":"Abstract\n                Single-cell large language models (scLLMs) capture essential biological insights from vast single-cell atlases but struggle in out-of-context applications, where zero-shot predictions can be unreliable. To address this, we introduce a single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework that integrates learnable, low-dimensional adapters into scLLMs. By freezing the backbone model and updating only the adapter parameters, scPEFT efficiently adapts to specific tasks using limited custom data. This approach mitigates catastrophic forgetting, reduces parameter tuning by over 96%, and decreases GPU memory usage by more than half, significantly enhancing scLLMs’s accessibility for resource-constrained researchers. Validated across diverse datasets, scPEFT outperformed zero-shot models and traditional fine-tuning in disease-specific, cross-species, and under-characterized cell population tasks. Its attention-mechanism analysis identified COVID-related genes associated with specific cell states and uncovered unique blood cell subpopulations, demonstrating scPEFT’s capacity for condition-specific interpretations. These findings position scPEFT as an efficient solution for improving scLLMs’ utilities in general single-cell analyses.","doi":"10.1101/2025.04.21.649754","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Machine …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000249","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Introduces scPEFT as a new framework for single-cell large language models with learnable adapters. The model is explicitly for single-cell biological data and fine-tunes scLLMs for downstream tasks.","evidence_snippet":"\"single-cell large language models (scLLMs)\" and \"single-cell parameter-efficient fine-tuning (scPEFT) framework\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000249","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes parameter-efficient fine-tuning of existing scLLMs rather than a new generative model. It mentions LLMs, but the candidate here is a tuning framework, not a foundation model itself.","evidence_snippet":"“parameter-efficient fine-tuning ... integrates learnable, low-dimensional adapters into scLLMs”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000250","title":"scpFormer: A Foundation Model for Unified Representation and Integration of the Single-Cell Proteomics","abstract":"The integration of single-cell proteomic data is often hindered by the fragmented nature of   , a transformer-based foundation model designed for single-cell proteomics. Pre-trained on","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"arXiv preprint arXiv …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000250","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Presents a transformer-based foundation model for single-cell proteomics. The abstract does not describe any substantive text or language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"a transformer-based foundation model designed for single-cell proteomics\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000250","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title and abstract identify scpFormer as a transformer-based foundation model and say it is pre-trained. The snippet does not clearly state a generative mechanism such as decoding, autoregression, or diffusion.","evidence_snippet":"“a transformer-based foundation model” and “Pre-trained on”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000253","title":"GSFM: A gene set foundation model pre-trained on a massive collection of diverse gene sets","abstract":"created from the Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO). More recently, single-cell transcriptomics  data  using an OpenAI top-performing large language model (LLM). Another model called","doi":"10.1016/j.patter.2026.101565","year":"2026","venue":"Patterns","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000253","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title indicates a gene set foundation model, but the record does not support a substantive text or language component. It is centered on biological gene sets rather than text-bio bridging.","evidence_snippet":"\"gene set foundation model pre-trained on a massive collection of diverse gene sets\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000253","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title says the model is a foundation model and the abstract mentions it is pre-trained on a massive collection of gene sets. The provided text does not clearly describe a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"“gene set foundation model pre-trained on a massive collection of diverse gene sets”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000254","title":"ChromFound: Towards A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data","abstract":"spatial and temporal gene expression patterns [31]. Identifying OCRs at singlecell level is   -scale pretrained deep language model for cell type annotation of single-cell rna-seq data.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Advances in …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000254","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"ChromFound is presented as a universal foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data. The record supports a biological modality but not a text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000254","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title presents ChromFound as a universal foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data. The abstract snippet does not give enough mechanism detail to confirm a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"“Towards A Universal Foundation Model for Single-Cell Chromatin Accessibiltiy Data”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000256","title":"AICellType: a large language model-based platform for accurate cell type annotation","abstract":"Abstract\n                  Accurate cell type annotation is critical for studying cellular heterogeneity in single-cell and spatial transcriptomics. However, existing methods largely rely on static gene markers, limiting adaptability to diverse biological contexts and data types. To overcome this limitation, we systematically benchmarked 79 large language models (LLMs) over 1130 single-cell and spatial transcriptomics datasets using an evaluation framework combining ontology structure and semantic reasoning to quantify model performance in biological relevance and annotation robustness. Claude 3.5 Sonnet achieved the best overall performance, balancing weighted accuracy (76%), robustness, inference speed, and cost-efficiency. Based on these findings, we developed AICellType (https://AICellType.jinlab.online), a free, open-source R package and web platform that integrates seamlessly with Seurat workflows, supports multiple species and tissues, and enables flexible model deployment via OpenRouter or custom APIs. By leveraging LLMs’ capacity to interpret marker–cell type associations, AICellType provides a scalable, efficient, and accessible solution for real-world cell annotation in both single-cell and spatial omics research.","doi":"10.1093/bib/bbag151","year":"2026","venue":"Briefings in …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000256","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract centers on benchmarking 79 large language models across 1130 transcriptomics datasets and releasing a platform. This is primarily a benchmark/resource and application platform paper, not a new candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically benchmarked 79 large language models\" and \"developed AICellType ... a free, open-source R package and web platform\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000256","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a benchmarking study over 79 LLMs and a deployed platform for cell type annotation. It does not present a new generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“we systematically benchmarked 79 large language models” and “developed AICellType ... a ... platform”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]
