y substantive text component.","evidence_snippet":"\"Foundation model representation ... for spatial gene expression prediction\""},{"record_id":"rec_000256","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract centers on benchmarking 79 large language models across 1130 transcriptomics datasets and releasing a platform. This is primarily a benchmark/resource and application platform paper, not a new candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically benchmarked 79 large language models\" and \"developed AICellType ... a free, open-source R package and web platform\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0208.stdout.log:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_001657","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a benchmarking study of BCR sequencing technologies and a generated reference resource, not a generative model paper. No foundation-model-like pretraining or transfer learning is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"benchmarking experiment\" and \"we have generated a resource for the immunology community\""},{"record_id":"rec_001658","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"This is a Comment on the landscape of single-cell large language models rather than a primary model report. It mentions scLLMs and benchmark studies, but does not describe a specific candidate model or its training.","evidence_snippet":"\"In this Comment, we examine the current landscape of scLLMs\""},{"record_id":"rec_001659","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"This reads as an editorial perspective on virtual cells, virtual organs, and foundation models, not a primary model paper. It mentions generative simulations and foundation models, but gives no concrete model architecture or training evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"predictive and generative simulations\" and \"embedding biological priors into foundation models\""},{"record_id":"rec_001660","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly calls scMSI a \"generative deep self-expression model,\" so the architecture is generative. It is presented as a task-specific multimodal integration model, with no clear foundation-model-style broad pretraining evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"contrastive and generative deep self-expression model\""},{"record_id":"rec_001661","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This paper uses fixed embeddings from pre-trained DNA language models with lightweight classifiers, so it is an inference wrapper rather than a generative model. The abstract discusses transferability of embeddings, but not a new foundation model candidate.","evidence_snippet":"\"embedding-based pipelines\" and \"extract fixed representations from these models and feed them into lightweight classifiers\""},{"record_id":"rec_001662","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"FEDKEA is a predictive enzyme-function annotation pipeline built on fine-tuned ESM-2 plus KNN, not a generative architecture. The abstract describes downstream classification and transfer learning, not foundation-model training evidence for a new candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"integrates ESM-2 and distance-weighted KNN\" and \"fine-tuned ESM-2 model\""},{"record_id":"rec_001663","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper is about DNA direct-coding and parallelized compression, which is not a generative text-bio model. The abstract focuses on encoding and decoding throughput rather than any model pretraining or transferable representation learning.","evidence_snippet":"\"two-bit coding\" and \"encoding and decoding efficiency\""},{"record_id":"rec_001664","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a review of droplet-based scRNA-seq technology, workflows, and applications, not a primary model paper. It only briefly mentions AI-driven analysis as a future frontier.","evidence_snippet":"\"This review synthesizes the technological evolution\" and \"AI-driven analysis of multimodal datasets\""}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/architecture_reviewer/batch_0482.prompt.txt:47:[{"record_id":"rec_003849","title":"scUnify: A Unified Framework for Zero-shot Inference of Single-Cell Foundation Models","abstract":"Abstract\n                \n                  Foundation models (FMs) pre-trained on large-scale single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data provide powerful cell embeddings, but their practical usability and systematic comparison are limited by model-specific environments, preprocessing pipelines, and execution procedures. To address these challenges, we introduce scUnify, a unified zero-shot inference framework for single-cell foundation models. scUnify accepts a standard AnnData object and automatically manages environment isolation, preprocessing, and tokenization through a registry-based modular design. It employs a hierarchical distributed inference strategy that combines Ray-based task scheduling with multi-GPU data-parallel execution via HuggingFace Accelerate, enabling scalable inference on datasets containing up to one million cells. In addition, built-in integration of scIB and scGraph metrics enables standardized cross-model embedding evaluation within a single workflow. Benchmarking results demonstrate substantial reductions in inference time compared with the original model implementations, while preserving embedding quality and achieving near-linear multi-GPU scaling. scUnify is implemented in Python and is publicly available at\n                  https://github.com/DHKim327/scUnify\n                  .","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003850","title":"Evaluating the learnability of single-cell large language models on multiple tasks","abstract":"Abstract\n                The rise of single-cell foundation models (scFMs) has sparked interest in their potential to unify diverse biological tasks. However, their practical utility and the validity of scaling laws—the assumption that performance improves with model and data size—remain under-examined. Here, we systematically evaluate two representative scFMs, Geneformer and scGPT, across perturbation prediction and cell type annotation tasks. Our findings suggest that the benefits of large-scale pretraining are strongly task-dependent, conferring substantial advantages in cell type annotation but limited gains in perturbation prediction. Furthermore, our results indicate that increasing model size does not guarantee improved performance and can even be detrimental, challenging the ``bigger is better'' paradigm. By comparing model performance on real versus synthetic data with different levels of complexity, our analysis suggests that for perturbation prediction, the tested scFMs capture little more than simple summary statistics and may struggle to learn complex biological interactions. These results highlight the need to move beyond scaling and toward developing models that integrate deeper biological knowledge. We suggest that a renewed focus on task-specific architectures and biologically-informed priors may be critical for unlocking the true potential of foundation models in single-cell biology.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003851","title":"Cell phenotypes in the biomedical literature: a systematic analysis and text mining corpus","abstract":"Abstract\n                The variety of cell phenotypes identified by single-cell technologies is rapidly expanding, yet this knowledge is dispersed across the scientific literature and incompletely represented in structured resources. We present the CellLink corpus, a manually annotated collection of over 22,000 mentions of human and mouse cell populations in recent journal articles, distinguishing specific cell phenotypes, heterogeneous cell populations, and vague cell populations, and linking to Cell Ontology (CL) terms as either exact or related matches, covering nearly half of the terms in the current CL. A systematic analysis reveals lineage-specific patterns in how authors utilize anatomical context, molecular signatures, functional roles, developmental stage, and other attributes in cell naming. We show that fine-tuning transformer-based models on CellLink yields strong performance for named entity recognition, while embedding-based approaches support zero-shot entity linking and distinguishing exact from related matches. We further demonstrate the utility of CellLink to expand and refine the chondrocyte branch of CL.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003852","title":"A Generative Foundation Model for Multimodal Histopathology","abstract":"in natural language prompts. We benchmarked MUPAD against three baseline models:   We employed Histoplus 36 to perform downstream cell-type composition analysis by classifying","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"arXiv preprint arXiv …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003853","title":"From Modality Translation to Regulatory Reasoning: Interpretable AI for Single-Cell Multi-Omic Systems","abstract":"Building on this foundation, we contribute to population- 42 ix 3.4 Determining cell type–specific  eQTLs from single-cell  commonly utilized in natural language processing models as well","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003854","title":"Learning to Cluster Rare Cell Types: Implicit Semantic Data Augmentation for Spatial Multi-modal Omics Analysis","abstract":"Spatial multi-modal omics technologies have transformed biological research by enabling the simultaneous profiling of gene expression, protein abundance, and chromatin accessibility within their native spatial contexts. Despite these advances, accurately clustering rare cell types remains a major challenge due to data sparsity, high dimensionality, and limited annotated samples. While Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) have shown potential in modeling spatial omics data, their effectiveness is often constrained by the use of fixed K-nearest neighbor (KNN) graph structures, which fail to capture latent semantic relationships masked by sequencing noise. To overcome these limitations, we propose CRCT (Clustering Rare Cell Types): a novel framework that combines Implicit Semantic Data Augmentation (ISDA) with adaptive graph learning for spatial multi-modal omics analysis. Unlike traditional augmentation strategies that generate explicit synthetic samples, CRCT operates in the deep feature space by dynamically estimating intra-class covariance matrices and implicitly perturbing features along semantically meaningful directions. This enables effective augmentation for rare cell populations while preserving biological fidelity. Extensive experiments across four real-world datasets (HLN, MB, Stereo‑CITE‑seq, and SPOTS) and one synthetic benchmark demonstrate the state-of-the-art performance of CRCT, achieving improvements of up to +1.7 NMI and +7.8 ARI over strong baseline methods.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Proceedings of the AAAI …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003855","title":"Advancing single-cell omics and cell-based therapeutics with quantum computing","abstract":"of single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data, cell-type  have been proposed for  performing natural language  models to complex generative and AI foundation models 125","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Reviews Molecular …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003856","title":"Modelling drug-induced cellular perturbation responses with a biologically informed dual-branch transformer","abstract":"architectures in single-cell large language models, we adopt  similar to word tokens in natural  language processing 51 , in  task that aims to classify the cell type based on the \\(< \\mathrm{","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Machine …","sources":["google_scholar"]}]
